lumns as:\n\n1. Ones (rightmost)\n2. Tens\n3. Hundreds\n4. Thousands\n5. Ten-thousands\n6. Hundred-thousands (leftmost)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nAfter adding the hundred-thousands place digits (4 and 6) and the carryover 1, we get 10 + 1 = 11. So we write 11 in the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands places. Wait, but how can two digits be in one place? That doesn't make sense. Each column can only have one digit. So actually, when adding the hundred-thousands place, we have:\n\n4 (hundred-thousands) + 6 (hundred-thousands) = 10. So we write 0 and carry 1. But since we are in the hundred-thousands place, the carryover 1 is added to the hundred-thousands place. Wait, but the hundred-thousands place is the sixth digit. So adding 4 + 6 = 10, we write 0 and carry 1. Then, since there are no more columns, we add the carryover 1 as a new digit in the hundred-thousands place. So the hundred-thousands place becomes 1 (from the carryover) and the ten-thousands place is 0. Wait, no. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, let's do the addition step by step, writing each column:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nColumn 1 (ones): 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8.\n\nColumn 2 (tens): 8 + 2 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nColumn 3 (hundreds): 1 + 9 = 10, plus carry 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nColumn 4 (thousands): 2 + 1 = 3, plus carry 1 = 4. Write 4, no carry.\n\nColumn 5 (ten-thousands): 8 + 9 = 17, plus carry 0 = 17. Write 7, carry 1.\n\nColumn 6 (hundred-thousands): 4 + 6 = 10, plus carry 1 = 11. Write 11.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nColumn 6: 11\n\nColumn 5: 7\n\nColumn 4: 4\n\nColumn 3: 1\n\nColumn 2: 0\n\nColumn 1: 8\n\nBut when writing the number, we need to arrange the digits from highest place value to lowest. So:\n\nDigit 6 (hundred-thousands): 11\n\nDigit 5 (ten-thousands): 7\n\nDigit 4 (thousands): 4\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 1\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 0\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 8\n\nSo the number is 11 (hundred-thousands and ten-thousands), 7 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, but that