ite down 9. \n\nHundreds place: first number's hundreds digit is 2, second number's hundreds digit is 1. 2 + 1 = 3. No carryover. \n\nTens place: 3 + 4 = 7. \n\nOnes place: 1 + 7 = 8. \n\nWait, so putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nThousands: 9\n\nTen-thousands: 7 (from 9 + 7 + 1 carryover)\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 (from 9 + 6 + 1 carryover)\n\nWait, but that would make the sum 579,378. Wait, no. Wait, let's list the digits:\n\nFrom right to left:\n\n1st digit (ones): 8\n\n2nd digit (tens): 7\n\n3rd digit (hundreds): 3\n\n4th digit (thousands): 9\n\n5th digit (ten-thousands): 7\n\n6th digit (hundred-thousands): 5\n\nSo the number is 5 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones). So 579,378. But that's not matching the previous results. \n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me try to write it step by step:\n\nFirst number: 990,231\n\nSecond number: 679,147\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with the ones place: 1 + 7 = 8\n\nTens place: 3 + 4 = 7\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nThousands place: 0 + 9 = 9\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 7 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\nBut since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 is added as a new digit. So the total is 1 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place is 1, ten-thousands is 6, thousands is 6, hundreds is 9, tens is 7, ones is 8. So the number is 166,978. Wait, 1 6 6 9 7 8. So 166,978. \n\nBut that's different from all previous results. What's going on here? \n\nWait, let's check this step-by-step addition again. \n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 1 + 7 = 8. Correct.\n\n2. Tens: 3 + 4 = 7. Correct.\n\n3. Hundreds: 2 + 1 = 3. Correct.\n\n4. Thousands: 0 + 9 = 9. Correct.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 7 = 16. So write 6, carry 1. Correct.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 6 + 1 (carry) 