n679,147: digits are 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones)\n\nAdding them:\n\nOnes: 1 + 7 = 8\n\nTens: 3 + 4 = 7\n\nHundreds: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nThousands: 0 + 9 = 9\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 7 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\nBut since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 is added as a new digit. So the sum is 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place is 1, ten-thousands is 6, thousands is 6, hundreds is 9, tens is 7, ones is 8. So the number is 166,978. \n\nBut according to the calculator, it's 1,669,378. So there's a clear discrepancy. \n\nWait, so where is the mistake here? Let me check the addition again. \n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digits. Let me write both numbers vertically aligned:\n\n      990231\n\n    +679147\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 + 7 = 8\n\n3 + 4 = 7\n\n2 + 1 = 3\n\n0 + 9 = 9\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n9 + 6 = 15\n\nSo, the sum is 15 in the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands place. But since we can't have two digits, we write 5 and carry over 1. So the hundred-thousands place becomes 1, and the ten-thousands place becomes 5. Then, the thousands place is 9. \n\nWait, but the ten-thousands place was 9 + 7 = 16, which we wrote 6 and carried over 1. So the ten-thousands digit is 6, and carryover 1. Then, the hundred-thousands place is 9 + 6 + 1 = 16, which we write 6 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, we add the carryover 1 as a new digit. So the hundred-thousands place is 1. \n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 6\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 3\n\nTens: 7\n\nOnes: 8\n\nSo the sum is 166,978. But according to the calculator, it's 1,669,378. \n\nWait, this is impossible. There must be a mistake in my column addition. Let me check once more. \n\nWait, perhaps I misaligned the digits. Le