      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 731,130 and 407,746. Hmm, adding these two numbers together. Alright, let me start by writing them down one above the other to align the digits properly. \n\nSo, 731,130\n+407,746\n________\n\nStarting from the rightmost digits, which are the ones place. Let me add them up. \n\nFirst, the ones place: 0 + 6. That's 6. Easy enough. I write down 6 and carry over 0. \n\nNext, the tens place: 3 + 4. That's 7. No carryover, so just 7. Down the 7. \n\nHundreds place: 1 + 7. That's 8. Still no carryover. So 8. \n\nThousands place: 1 + 0. That's 1. No problem. \n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 4. That's 7. \n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 + 4. That's 11. Wait, hold on. Since we're adding two digits here, 7 and 4, that gives 11. So we write down 1 and carry over 1 to the next column. \n\nBut wait, the second number doesn't have a digit in the hundred-thousands place. So the carried over 1 will just be added there. So the total is 11 in the hundred-thousands place. \n\nPutting it all together: 11 (from hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 117,876. \n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe I should add them another way to verify. \n\nAlternatively, I can break down each number into its components and add them separately. \n\n731,130 can be broken down as:\n700,000 + 30,000 + 1,000 + 100 + 30.\n\nSimilarly, 407,746 is:\n400,000 + 7,000 + 700 + 40 + 6.\n\nNow, adding the thousands place: 1,000 + 7,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds place: 1,000 + 700 = 1,700. Wait, no. Wait, 731,130 has 1,000 and 100, while 407,746 has 700 and 40. So adding those: 1,000 + 700 = 1,700 and 100 + 40 = 140. So total for hundreds place is 1,700 + 140 = 1,840.\n\nTens place: 30 + 40 = 70.\n\nOnes place: 30 + 6 = 36.\n\nNow, adding all these components:\n\n700,000 + 400,000 = 1,100,000.\n\n1,000 + 7,000 = 8,000.\n\n1,000 + 700 = 1,700.\n\n100 + 40 = 140.\n\n30 + 6