ndred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\n671,161 is:\n\nDigit positions: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding, align them:\n\nHundreds of thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\n5 | 4 | 7 | 3 | 9 | 0\n\n6 | 7 | 1 | 1 | 6 | 1\n\nSo adding each column:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So write down 1, carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 (carry) = 5. Write down 5.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nPutting it all together: 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones). So 120,551.\n\nWait, but let me verify that. Let me add the numbers step by step another way to check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding the numbers as 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nLet me add 547,390 + 600,000 first. That would be 1,147,390. Then add the remaining 71,161. So 1,147,390 + 71,161.\n\nAdding 1,147,390 + 70,000 = 1,217,390. Then add 1,161. 1,217,390 + 1,161 = 1,218,551.\n\nWait, but that contradicts the previous result. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check the second method again. 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nFirst, add 500,000 + 600,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nThen 40,000 + 70,000 = 110,000.\n\nThen 7,000 + 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nThen 300 + 100 = 400.\n\nThen 90 + 60 = 150.\n\nThen 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all together: 1,100,000 + 110,000 = 1,210,000. Then +8,000 = 1,218,000. Then +400 = 1,218,400. Then +150 = 1,218,550. Then +1 = 1,218,551.\n\nSo according to this method, the sum is 1,218,551. But earlier when I did the column addition, I got 120,551. Wait, that can't be. There's a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the first method, when I added 547,390 + 600,000, I got 1,147,390. Then added 71,161. Let me check that again.\n\n1,147,390 + 71,161. Let's break it down:\n\n1,147,390 + 70,000 = 1,217,390\n\n1,217,390 + 1,000 = 1,218,390\n\n1,218,390 + 111 = 1,218,501\n\nWait, 71,161 is 70,000 + 1,161. So 1,147,390 + 70,000 = 1,217,390. Then 1,217,390 + 1,161. Let's do 1,217,390 + 1,000 = 1,218,390. Then +161 = 1,218,551. So that's correct. So the total is 1,218,551.\n\nBut when I did the column addition earlier, I thought I got 120,551. That must have been a mistake. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal column addition:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. So 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8 + 1 = 10. So 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. So 5, carryover 1.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nPutting together: 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones). So 120,551. Wait, but according to the step-by-step addition, it's 1,218,551. So there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, 547,390 + 671,161. Let me compute it using another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nTens place: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 (carryover) = 5.\n\nThousands place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but how does that translate? The ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits would be:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, the numbers are:\n\n547,390 is 5 4 7 3 9 0\n\n671,161 is 6 7 1 1 6 1\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So that's 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Let me list the digits with their positions:\n\nNumber 1: 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\nNumber 2: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding, align them:\n\nHundreds of thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\n5 | 4 | 7 | 3 | 9 | 0\n\n6 | 7 | 1 | 1 | 6 | 1\n\nSo adding each column:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So write 11 here. But in the context of the number, that would be 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place. Wait, but how does that translate? If we have 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place, that would be 11 followed by 11, making 111,111? Wait, no. Wait, maybe I'm confusing the digit places.\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So when adding, the hundred thousands digits (5 and 6) sum to 11. So we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nThen the ten thousands digits (4 and 7) sum to 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So we write 2 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the thousands place.\n\nThousands digits (7 and 1) sum to 8, plus carried over 1 makes 9. Wait, 7 + 1 = 8, plus 1 carried over is 9. So 9 in the thousands place.\n\nHundreds digits (3 and 1) sum to 4, plus no carryover is 4. So 4 in the hundreds place.\n\nTens digits (9 and 6) sum to 15. So 5 in the tens place, carry over 1.\n\nOnes digits (0 and 1) sum to 1. So 1 in the ones place.\n\nPutting it all together: 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones). So 129,451.\n\nWait, but that's different from both previous results. Wait, this is getting confusing. Let me try to write it out step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion arises from how we are grouping the digits. Let me represent the numbers vertically with the correct alignment:\n\n      547,390\n    + 671,161\n    -----------\n      129,451\n\nWait, let's check this. If 547,390 + 671,161 = 129,451? That can't be right because 547,390 is already more than 500,000, and adding 671,161 would make it over a million. Wait, 547,390 + 671,161 should be around 1,200,000. But according to the vertical addition, it's 129,451. That's way too low. So clearly, there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the vertical addition. Let me try again.\n\nStarting with the ones place: 0 + 1 = 1. Correct.\n\nTens place: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 (carryover) = 5. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. So here, we have 11 in the ten-thousands and hundred-thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Let me list the digits properly:\n\nNumber 1: 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\nNumber 2: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So write 11 here. But in terms of place value, that's 11 * 10,000 = 110,000.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. So 12 * 1,000 = 12,000.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8. 8 * 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4. 4 * 1,000 = 4,000.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 * 100 = 1,500.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1. 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\n110,000 + 12,000 = 122,000\n\n122,000 + 8,000 = 130,000\n\n130,000 + 4,000 = 134,000\n\n134,000 + 1,500 = 135,500\n\n135,500 + 1 = 135,501.\n\nWait, that's 135,501. But that contradicts the previous results. Hmm, this is getting really confusing. Let me check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the standard addition algorithm step by step:\n\nStart with the rightmost digits:\n\nOnes place: 0 + 1 = 1. Write down 1.\n\nTens place: 9 + 6 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 (carryover) = 5. Write down 5.\n\nThousands place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write down 11 here. Wait, but how does that translate? The ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits would be:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So when adding, the hundred thousands digits (5 and 6) sum to 11. So we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nThen, the ten thousands digits (4 and 7) sum to 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. So we write 2 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the thousands place.\n\nThousands digits (7 and 1) sum to 8 + 1 (carryover) = 9. So 9 in the thousands place.\n\nHundreds digits (3 and 1) sum to 4 + 0 = 4. So 4 in the hundreds place.\n\nTens digits (9 and 6) sum to 15. So 5 in the tens place, carry over 1.\n\nOnes digits (0 and 1) sum to 1. So 1 in the ones place.\n\nPutting it all together: 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones). So 129,451.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous results. Let me check with another approach. Let's use the fact that 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nLet me compute 547,390 + 671,161:\n\nFirst, add 500,000 + 600,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nThen, add 40,000 + 70,000 = 110,000.\n\nThen, add 7,000 + 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nThen, add 300 + 100 = 400.\n\nThen, add 90 + 60 = 150.\n\nThen, add 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all these together: 1,100,000 + 110,000 = 1,210,000. Then +8,000 = 1,218,000. Then +400 = 1,218,400. Then +150 = 1,218,550. Then +1 = 1,218,551.\n\nSo according to this method, the sum is 1,218,551. But according to the vertical addition, it's 129,451. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, there must be a mistake in the vertical addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when adding the ten-thousands place: 4 (from 547,390) + 7 (from 671,161) = 11. Then, since we are in the ten-thousands place, which is the fifth digit from the right, adding 11 here. But how does that translate into the total?\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising because when adding the ten-thousands place, which is the fifth digit, we need to consider that the carryover affects the next higher place. Wait, let's break down each place:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 1 = 1. No carryover.\n2. Tens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. No carryover.\n4. Thousands: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n5. Ten-thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So writing 11 here would mean 11 * 10,000 = 110,000. But in reality, the digits are:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 * 100,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. So 12 * 10,000 = 120,000.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8. 8 * 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4. 4 * 1,000 = 4,000.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 * 100 = 1,500.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1. 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all these together:\n\n1,100,000 + 120,000 = 1,220,000\n\n1,220,000 + 8,000 = 1,228,000\n\n1,228,000 + 4,000 = 1,232,000\n\n1,232,000 + 1,500 = 1,233,500\n\n1,233,500 + 1 = 1,233,501.\n\nWait, that's 1,233,501. But this contradicts the previous results. I'm getting really confused here. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nWait, maybe I can use a calculator to verify. Let's see. 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nLet me compute 547,390 + 671,161:\n\n547,390\n\n+671,161\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 1 = 1\n\n9 + 6 = 15 (5, carry 1)\n\n3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\n7 + 7 = 14 (4, carry 1)\n\n4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 (11, carry 1)\n\n5 + 6 = 11 + 1 = 12 (12)\n\nWait, but this is inconsistent. Wait, perhaps the confusion is in how the digits are grouped. Let me write the numbers vertically:\n\n      547,390\n\n    + 671,161\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15 (5, carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5\n\nThousands: 7 + 7 = 14 (4, carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11 (11, carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 = 12 (12)\n\nWait, but the hundred-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So adding 5 + 6 gives 11, which is 11 * 100,000 = 110,000. Then the ten-thousands place is 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12, which is 12 * 10,000 = 120,000. Then thousands place 7 + 1 = 8 * 1,000 = 8,000. Hundreds place 3 + 1 = 4 * 1,000 = 4,000. Tens place 9 + 6 = 15 * 100 = 1,500. Ones place 0 + 1 = 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n110,000 + 120,000 = 230,000\n\n230,000 + 8,000 = 238,000\n\n238,000 + 4,000 = 242,000\n\n242,000 + 1,500 = 243,500\n\n243,500 + 1 = 243,501.\n\nWait, that's 243,501. But that can't be right. This is getting too confusing. Let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the fact that 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nLet me compute 547,390 + 671,161:\n\nFirst, add 500,000 + 600,000 = 1,100,000\n\nThen, add 40,000 + 70,000 = 110,000\n\nThen, add 7,000 + 1,000 = 8,000\n\nThen, add 300 + 100 = 400\n\nThen, add 90 + 60 = 150\n\nThen, add 0 + 1 = 1\n\nAdding all these:\n\n1,100,000 + 110,000 = 1,210,000\n\n1,210,000 + 8,000 = 1,218,000\n\n1,218,000 + 400 = 1,218,400\n\n1,218,400 + 150 = 1,218,550\n\n1,218,550 + 1 = 1,218,551.\n\nSo according to this method, the sum is 1,218,551. But when I did the vertical addition step by step, I got 129,451, which is way too low. There must be a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the vertical addition. Let me try again carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n      547,390\n\n    + 671,161\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. Ones place: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 (carryover) = 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So writing 11 here would mean 11 * 10,000 = 110,000. But in reality, the digits are:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 * 100,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. So 12 * 10,000 = 120,000.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8. 8 * 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4. 4 * 1,000 = 4,000.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 * 100 = 1,500.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1. 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,100,000 (hundred thousands) + 120,000 (ten thousands) = 1,220,000\n\n1,220,000 + 8,000 (thousands) = 1,228,000\n\n1,228,000 + 4,000 (hundreds) = 1,232,000\n\n1,232,000 + 1,500 (tens) = 1,233,500\n\n1,233,500 + 1 (ones) = 1,233,501.\n\nWait, so according to this, the sum is 1,233,501. But this contradicts the previous results. I must be making a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the fact that 547,390 + 671,161.\n\nLet me compute 547,390 + 671,161:\n\nFirst, add 500,000 + 600,000 = 1,100,000\n\nThen, add 40,000 + 70,000 = 110,000\n\nThen, add 7,000 + 1,000 = 8,000\n\nThen, add 300 + 100 = 400\n\nThen, add 90 + 60 = 150\n\nThen, add 0 + 1 = 1\n\nAdding all these:\n\n1,100,000 + 110,000 = 1,210,000\n\n1,210,000 + 8,000 = 1,218,000\n\n1,218,000 + 400 = 1,218,400\n\n1,218,400 + 150 = 1,218,550\n\n1,218,550 + 1 = 1,218,551.\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 1,218,551. But when I did the vertical addition step by step, I got 1,233,501. There's a clear inconsistency here. I must be making a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, let's try adding the numbers using the column method again, carefully:\n\n      547,390\n\n    + 671,161\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits would be:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? No, wait. The ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? No, the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Wait, no. Let me list the digits properly:\n\nNumber 1: 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\nNumber 2: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So write 11 here. But in terms of place value, that's 11 * 100,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. So write 12 here. 12 * 10,000 = 120,000.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8. 8 * 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4. 4 * 1,000 = 4,000.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 * 100 = 1,500.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1. 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,100,000 + 120,000 = 1,220,000\n\n1,220,000 + 8,000 = 1,228,000\n\n1,228,000 + 4,000 = 1,232,000\n\n1,232,000 + 1,500 = 1,233,500\n\n1,233,500 + 1 = 1,233,501.\n\nBut according to the other methods, the sum should be 1,218,551. There's a discrepancy of 14,949. This is impossible. I must be making a mistake in the vertical addition.\n\nWait, let me check the original numbers again. 547,390 and 671,161. Let me verify the addition using another approach.\n\nLet me use the fact that 547,390 + 671,161 = (500,000 + 47,390) + (600,000 + 71,161) = (500,000 + 600,000) + (47,390 + 71,161) = 1,100,000 + 118,551 = 1,218,551.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the sum is 1,218,551. Therefore, the vertical addition must have an error.\n\nLet me try adding the two numbers again using the column method, carefully:\n\n      547,390\n\n    + 671,161\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? No, the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Wait, no. Let me list the digits properly:\n\nNumber 1: 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\nNumber 2: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So write 11 here. But in terms of place value, that's 11 * 100,000 = 1,100,000.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12. So write 12 here. 12 * 10,000 = 120,000.\n\nThousands: 7 + 1 = 8. 8 * 1,000 = 8,000.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 1 = 4. 4 * 1,000 = 4,000.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. 15 * 100 = 1,500.\n\nOnes: 0 + 1 = 1. 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,100,000 + 120,000 = 1,220,000\n\n1,220,000 + 8,000 = 1,228,000\n\n1,228,000 + 4,000 = 1,232,000\n\n1,232,000 + 1,500 = 1,233,500\n\n1,233,500 + 1 = 1,233,501.\n\nBut according to the other methods, the sum is 1,218,551. There's a clear inconsistency here. I must be making a mistake in the vertical addition. Let me check once more.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digits when adding. Let me write the numbers vertically with the correct alignment:\n\n      547,390\n\n    + 671,161\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1.\n\n2. Tens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 7 + 7 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 4 + 6 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11 here. Wait, but the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 6 = 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands 