maybe I made a mistake in the initial problem statement. Let me check again. The problem says: What is the sum of 664196 and 575329?\n\nWait, 664,196 and 575,329. Let me write them out:\n\n664,196\n\n575,329\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 + 9 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\n9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\n1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, carry 0\n\n4 + 5 = 9 + 0 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n\n6 + 7 = 13 + 0 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n6 + 5 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 0\n\nSo the result is 12 (hundred-thousands and ten-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). So 123,5925? Wait, no. Wait, 12 in hundred-thousands and ten-thousands is 120,000. Then 3 in ten-thousands is 30,000. 5 in thousands is 5,000. 9 in hundreds is 900. 2 in tens is 20. 5 in ones is 5. So 120,000 + 30,000 = 150,000; 150,000 + 5,000 = 155,000; 155,000 + 900 = 155,900; 155,900 + 20 = 155,920; 155,920 + 5 = 155,925. But according to the other method, it's 1,239,525. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let's check:\n\nFirst, 664,196 + 500,000 = 1,164,196. Correct.\n\nThen, 1,164,196 + 75,329. Let's break down 75,329 into 70,000 + 5,000 + 300 + 20 + 9.\n\n1,164,196 + 70,000 = 1,234,196. Correct.\n\n1,234,196 + 5,000 = 1,239,196. Correct.\n\n1,239,196 + 300 = 1,239,496. Correct.\n\n1,239,496 + 20 = 1,239,516. Correct.\n\n1,239,516 + 9 = 1,239,525. Correct.\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 1,239,525. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 155,925. That can't be. There's a mistake here. Wait, 155,925 is 155 thousand, but 1,239,525 is 1 million 239 thousand 525. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred-thousands place: 6 + 5 = 11. Then we have a carryover of 1 from the ten-thousands place. So 11 + 1 = 12. So we write 2 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, we add that 1 as a new digit. So the total sum is 12 (hundred-thousands and ten-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). So 123,5925? Wait, no. Wait, 12 in hundred-thousands and ten-thousands is 120,000. Then 3 in ten-thousands is 30,000. 5 in thousands is 5,000. 9 in hundreds is 900. 2 in tens is 20. 5 in ones is 5. So 120,000 + 30,000 = 150,000; 150,000 + 5,000 = 155,000; 155,000 + 900 = 155,900; 155,900 + 20 = 155,920; 155,920 + 5 = 155,925. But according to the other method, it's 1,239,525. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the initial problem statement. Let me check the numbers again. The problem says 664196 and 575329. Let me verify the digits:\n\n664,196: 6 hundred thousands, 6 ten thousands, 4 thousands, 1 hundred, 9 tens, 6 ones.\n\n575,329: 5 hundred thousands, 7 ten thousands, 5 thousands, 3 hundreds, 2 tens, 9 ones.\n\nYes, that's correct. So adding them:\n\n664,196 + 575,329.\n\nLet me use another method: adding each digit separately with proper alignment.\n\nLet's write the numbers vertically:\n\n      664,196\n    + 575,329\n    -----------\n      123,525\n\nWait, but according to this, the sum is 123,525. But according to the other methods, it's 1,239,525. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe the alignment is wrong. Let me check:\n\nIf we write:\n\n      664,196\n    + 575,329\n    -----------\n      123,525\n\nBut 664,196 + 575,329 = 1,239,525, not 123,525. So the alignment is wrong. The 123,525 would be if we added 664 + 575 and 196 + 329, but that's not the case here. So the vertical addition must have been done incorrectly.\n\nWait, let me try adding the numbers again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 6 + 9 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, carry 0.\n\nThousands place: 4 + 5 = 9 + 0 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0.\n\nTen-thousands place: 6 + 7 = 13 + 0 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 + 5 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 0.\n\nSo the sum is 2 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). So 235,925. Wait, but that's 235,925. But according to the other method, it's 1,239,525. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the hundred-thousands place. Let me check:\n\nFirst number: 664,196. The digits are:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 6\n\n- Ten thousands: 6\n\n- Thousands: 4\n\n- Hundreds: 1\n\n- Tens: 9\n\n- Ones: 6\n\nSecond number: 575,329.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 5\n\n- Ten thousands: 7\n\n- Thousands: 5\n\n- Hundreds: 3\n\n- Tens: 2\n\n- Ones: 9\n\nAdding hundred thousands: 6 + 5 = 11. Carryover 1.\n\nAdding ten thousands: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 (carry) = 14. Wait, hold on! Wait, in the ten-thousands place, we have 6 + 7 = 13, but we also have a carryover of 1 from the thousands place. So 13 + 1 = 14. So we write 4 and carryover 1.\n\nWait, this is the mistake! Earlier, I didn't account for the carryover from the thousands place to the ten-thousands place. So let's redo the addition step by step with this correction.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones place: 6 + 9 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 1 + 3 = 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 5, carry 0.\n\n4. Thousands place: 4 + 5 = 9 + 0 = 9 \u2192 9, carry 0.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 6 + 7 = 13 + 0 = 13 + 1 (carryover from hundreds place) \u2192 14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 6 + 5 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 0.\n\nSo the sum is 2 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the ten-thousands place is 4, carryover 1 to the hundred-thousands place. So the hundred-thousands place is 6 + 5 + 1 = 12. So we write 2 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, we add that 1 as a new digit. So the total sum is 1 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands), 4 (ten-