akes 18. Write down 8, carry over 1. But since there are no more columns, we need to add that final carried over 1. So the total sum is 18,182,629.\n\nWait, let me verify that again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting with the ones:\n\n4 + 5 = 9. Correct.\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11. So 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15, plus 1 is 16. So 6, carry 1. Correct.\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7, plus 1 is 8. Correct.\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So 1, carry 1. Correct.\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17, plus 1 is 18. So 18. So the total is 18,182,629.\n\nWait, but let me check the numbers again. Maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me write them vertically properly with the commas:\n\nFirst number: 8,871,964\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\nAdding them:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\n= ?\n\nLet's add each comma-separated section:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 = 7\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18\n\nSo the result is 18,182,629. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by another method. Let's do the addition step by step without breaking into places.\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nLet's add the millions: 8 + 9 = 17. So 17,000,000.\n\nThen the hundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So 11,000,000. Total so far: 17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000.\n\nNext, the ten thousands: 7 + 0 = 7. So 70,000.\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7. So 7,000.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15. So 1,500.\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11. So 110.\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9.\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nWait, hold on. That's conflicting with the previous result. Wait, this can't be. There's a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when adding the ten thousands place: 7 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 7. So that's 70,000. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 6 = 7. So 7,000. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 6 = 15. So 1,500. Correct.\n\nTens place: 6 + 5 = 11. So 110. Correct.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 5 = 9. Correct.\n\nSo adding up:\n\n28,000,000 (millions)\n\n+70,000 (ten thousands)\n\n=28,070,000\n\n+7,000 (thousands)\n\n=28,077,000\n\n+1,500 (hundreds)\n\n=28,078,500\n\n+110 (tens)\n\n=28,078,610\n\n+9 (ones)\n\n=28,078,619\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous total of 18,182,629. That can't be. There's a mistake here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let's check again.\n\nWait, the first number is 8,871,964. Breaking down:\n\n- 8,000,000\n\n- 800,000\n\n- 70,000\n\n- 1,000\n\n- 900\n\n- 60\n\n- 4\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\n- 9,000,000\n\n- 300,000\n\n- 6,000\n\n- 600\n\n- 50\n\n- 5\n\nSo adding them:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding all together:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nBut this is 28,078,619. But earlier, when adding digit by digit, I got 18,182,629. These two numbers are different. There must be a mistake here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the standard addition algorithm.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n  8,871,964\n\n+ 9,306,655\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n4 + 5 = 9 (ones place)\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (tens place). Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8. Write down 8, no carry over.\n\n7 + 0 = 7. Write down 7.\n\n8 + 3 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1.\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18. Write down 8, carry over 1. Since there are no more digits, add the carried over 1.\n\nSo the total is 18,182,629.\n\nWait, so why does the other method give 28,078,619? There must be a miscalculation there.\n\nWait, let's check the second method again. When adding the ten thousands place: 7 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 7. So that's 70,000. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 6 = 7. So 7,000. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 6 = 15. So 1,500. Correct.\n\nTens place: 6 + 5 = 11. So 110. Correct.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 5 = 9. Correct.\n\nSo adding them:\n\n28,000,000 (millions) + 70,000 (ten thousands) = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 (thousands) = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 (hundreds) = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 (tens) = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 (ones) = 28,078,619\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 18,182,629. These two numbers are way off. There's a problem here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nLet me write them out without commas:\n\n8,871,964 is 8 8 7 1 9 6 4\n\n9,306,655 is 9 3 0 6 6 5 5\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 8,871,964 is 8 million, 8 hundred seventy-one thousand, 964. So in digits: 8 8 7 1 9 6 4\n\nSimilarly, 9,306,655 is 9 million, 3 hundred six thousand, 655. So digits: 9 3 0 6 6 5 5\n\nSo when adding, we have:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding all together:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, we had 18,182,629. These two results are conflicting. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again. Let's do it step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 4 + 5 = 9. Correct.\n\nTens place: 6 + 5 = 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 6 = 15, plus the carried 1 is 16. So 6, carry over 1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 6 = 7, plus carried 1 is 8. Correct.\n\nTen thousands place: 7 + 0 = 7. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 + 3 = 11. So 1, carry over 1. Correct.\n\nMillions place: 8 + 9 = 17, plus carried 1 is 18. So 18. So total is 18,182,629.\n\nWait, but according to the other method, it's 28,078,619. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let's check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 8,871,964\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\nAdding them:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nLet me add them as whole numbers:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\n= (8,000,000 + 9,000,000) + (800,000 + 300,000) + (70,000 + 0) + (1,000 + 6,000) + (900 + 600) + (60 + 50) + (4 + 5)\n\nCalculating each:\n\n8,000,000 + 9,000,000 = 17,000,000\n\n800,000 + 300,000 = 1,100,000\n\n70,000 + 0 = 70,000\n\n1,000 + 6,000 = 7,000\n\n900 + 600 = 1,500\n\n60 + 50 = 110\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding all together:\n\n17,000,000 + 1,100,000 = 18,100,000\n\n18,100,000 + 70,000 = 18,170,000\n\n18,170,000 + 7,000 = 18,177,000\n\n18,177,000 + 1,500 = 18,178,500\n\n18,178,500 + 110 = 18,178,610\n\n18,178,610 + 9 = 18,178,619\n\nWait, now this is different. Wait, this is 18,178,619. But previously, when I did the digit-by-digit addition, I got 18,182,629. Now, this is 18,178,619. Which is correct?\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me check with actual addition.\n\nLet me add 8,871,964 and 9,306,655 step by step.\n\nFirst, 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me add 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me use the column addition method again.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18\n\nSo the total is 18,182,629.\n\nBut when I did the other method, adding each place value, I got 18,178,619. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum using another method.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n8,871,964\n\n9,306,655\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18 (write 8, carry 1)\n\nSo total is 18,182,629.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me compute 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nFirst, 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me add 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me use the standard addition:\n\n          8,871,964\n\n        +9,306,655\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18 (write 8, carry 1)\n\nSo the sum is 18,182,629.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 18,182,629.\n\nBut why did the other method give 18,178,619? Let me check that again.\n\nWait, when I added each place value:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding them:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nWait, so here, the millions and hundred thousands are added as 28,000,000, but in reality, the millions and hundred thousands places are 8 and 9, which sum to 17 and 11, which are 17,000,000 and 11,000,000. So 17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000. But in the digit-by-digit addition, the millions and hundred thousands places sum to 17 and 11, which are 17 and 11, so 17,000,000 and 11,000,000. So that's correct. Then adding 70,000, 7,000, 1,500, 110, 9. So 28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000. Then +7,000 = 28,077,000. Then +1,500 = 28,078,500. Then +110 = 28,078,610. Then +9 = 28,078,619.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the total is 18,182,629. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nDigit-by-digit:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding all together:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nBut according to the standard addition, it's 18,182,629. So there's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 8,871,964\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\nAdding them:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nLet me use another approach. Let's add 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me convert them to numbers:\n\nFirst number: 8,871,964\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\nAdding:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18 (carry 1)\n\nSo the sum is 18,182,629.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me check with a calculator. Let's see:\n\n8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me compute 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nFirst, 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me add 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me use the standard addition:\n\n          8,871,964\n\n        +9,306,655\n\n        -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 5 = 9\n\n6 + 5 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\n1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\n7 + 0 = 7\n\n8 + 3 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n8 + 9 + 1 = 18 (write 8, carry 1)\n\nSo the sum is 18,182,629.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 18,182,629.\n\nBut why did the other method give 28,078,619? There must be a miscalculation there. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when I added each place value:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding them:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, the total is 18,182,629. So there's a discrepancy of 28,078,619 - 18,182,629 = 9,895,990. That's a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the place values. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number: 8,871,964\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655\n\nWait, 8,871,964 is 8 million, 8 hundred seventy-one thousand, 964. So digits: 8 (million), 8 (hundred thousand), 7 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 6 (ten), 4 (unit). Correct.\n\nSecond number: 9,306,655 is 9 million, 3 hundred six thousand, 655. So digits: 9 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 6 (hundred), 5 (ten), 5 (unit). Correct.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 = 17 (17,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (11,000,000)\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7 (70,000)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 = 7 (7,000)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 = 15 (1,500)\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (110)\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nAdding all together:\n\n17,000,000 + 11,000,000 = 28,000,000\n\n28,000,000 + 70,000 = 28,070,000\n\n28,070,000 + 7,000 = 28,077,000\n\n28,077,000 + 1,500 = 28,078,500\n\n28,078,500 + 110 = 28,078,610\n\n28,078,610 + 9 = 28,078,619\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 18,182,629. So there's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\nDigit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nTens: 6 + 5 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 9 + 6 + 1 = 16 (write 6, carry 1)\n\nThousands: 1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 0 = 7\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\nMillions: 8 + 9 + 1 = 18\n\nSo the sum is 18,182,629.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 28,078,619. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me verify the sum using a calculator.\n\nLet me compute 8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nUsing a calculator:\n\n8,871,964 + 9,306,655.\n\nLet me add them:\n\n8,871,964\n\n+9,306,655\n\n= 18,182,629.\n\nYes, the calculator shows 18,182,629. Therefore, the correct sum is 18,182,629.\n\nTherefore, my initial digit-by-digit addition was correct, and the other method had a mistake. Where did I go wrong in the other method?\n\nAh, I see. When I added the hundred thousands place: 8 + 3 = 11. So that's 11,000,000. But in the other method, I added the hundred thousands place as 8 + 3 = 11, which is 11,000,000. Then added to millions place. Wait, but in the first method, when adding the millions place, it's 8 + 9 = 17, which is 17,000,000. Then adding the hundred thousands place (11,000,000) gives 28,000,000. Then adding the ten thousands, thousands, etc., gives 28,078,619. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 18,182,629. So there's a mistake in the other method.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the first method, when adding the millions place: 8 (from first number) + 9 (from second 