      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the sum of two numbers: 991,841 and 139,758. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. I guess I can just add them directly. Let me write them down one above the other to align the digits properly. \n\nFirst, let me write both numbers with the same number of digits by adding leading zeros if necessary. 991,841 is already six digits, and 139,758 is also six digits. So they are both aligned like this:\n\n  991,841\n+ 139,758\n----------\n\nNow, I need to add each column starting from the rightmost digit. Let me start with the units place.\n\nStarting with the units digit: 1 (from 991,841) + 8 (from 139,758) = 9. That's straightforward. So the units digit of the sum is 9.\n\nMoving to the tens place: 4 + 5 = 9. Another simple addition. Tens place is 9.\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 7 = 15. Hmm, 8 comes from the first number and 7 from the second. So 8 + 7 is 15. I need to carry over the 1 to the next column.\n\nThousands place: 9 + 9 = 18, but wait, there's a carryover of 1 from the previous step. So 18 + 1 = 19. So the thousands place is 9, and carry over another 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. So that's 3 in the ten-thousands place and another carryover of 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 + 1 = 10, plus the carryover 1 makes 11. So that's 11 in the hundred-thousands place.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,113,699. Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the carries.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 1 + 8 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 4 + 5 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 8 + 7 = 15. Carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19. So 9, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. So 3, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11. So 11 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo the total is 11 (hundred-thousands) 13 (ten-thousands) 9 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 9 (tens) 9 (units). Wait, no, hold on. Wait, the places are:\n\nHundred-thousands, ten-thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units.\n\nWait, no. Let me list them properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 9\n\n2. Tens: 9\n\n3. Hundreds: 9\n\n4. Thousands: 9\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 3\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, when we added the hundred-thousands place, that's the sixth digit from the right. Let me structure it step by step:\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically with the correct alignment:\n\n      9 9 1 8 4 1\n+     1 3 9 7 5 8\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1 (units) + 8 (units) = 9\n\nTens place: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 7 = 15 (write down 5, carry over 1)\n\nThousands place: 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 (carry) = 19 (write down 9, carry over 1)\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13 (write down 3, carry over 1)\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11\n\nSo the result is 11 (hundred-thousands) 13 (ten-thousands) 9 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 9 (tens) 9 (units). Wait, but how does that translate?\n\nWait, the hundred-thousands place is the sixth digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nHundred-thousands: 11\n\nTen-thousands: 13\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 9\n\nUnits: 9\n\nWait, that seems confusing. Let me write the sum step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 9\n\n2. Tens: 9\n\n3. Hundreds: 9\n\n4. Thousands: 9\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 3\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 1\n\nSo when we write the number, it's 1 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 9 (units). Wait, no. Wait, the ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. So the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition 6: Hundred-thousands\n\nPosition 5: Ten-thousands\n\nPosition 4: Thousands\n\nPosition 3: Hundreds\n\nPosition 2: Tens\n\nPosition 1: Units\n\nSo the sum is:\n\nPosition 6: 1\n\nPosition 5: 1\n\nPosition 4: 3\n\nPosition 3: 9\n\nPosition 2: 6\n\nPosition 1: 9\n\nTherefore, the number is 113,699. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, 1 (hundred-thousands) + 1 (ten-thousands) + 3 (thousands) + 9 (hundreds) + 6 (tens) + 9 (units). So 113,699. But let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard addition algorithm step by step:\n\nLet me write the numbers:\n\n      991,841\n\n    +139,758\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units: 1 + 8 = 9\n\n2. Tens: 4 + 5 = 9\n\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 7 = 15 (write 5, carry 1)\n\n4. Thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 (carry) = 19 (write 9, carry 1)\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11\n\nSo the sum is 113,699. Wait, but 113,699 is 113 thousand and 699. Let me verify by another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers:\n\n991,841 + 139,758.\n\nLet me add 991,841 + 100,000 = 1,091,841\n\nThen add 39,758 to that: 1,091,841 + 39,758.\n\n1,091,841 + 30,000 = 1,121,841\n\n1,121,841 + 9,758 = 1,131,599\n\nWait, that gives 1,131,599. Hmm, that's different from 113,699. Wait, something's wrong here.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 991,841 + 139,758. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 991,841 + 139,758. Let's do it step by step:\n\nFirst, 991,841 + 100,000 = 1,091,841\n\nThen, 1,091,841 + 30,000 = 1,121,841\n\n1,121,841 + 9,000 = 1,130,841\n\n1,130,841 + 700 = 1,131,541\n\n1,131,541 + 50 = 1,131,591\n\n1,131,591 + 8 = 1,131,599\n\nWait, so according to this step-by-step addition, the sum is 1,131,599. But according to my initial column addition, I got 113,699. That's a discrepancy. There must be a mistake in my first method.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 991,841 + 139,758. Let me check with another approach. Let's use the fact that 991,841 is 1,000,000 - 899, and 139,758 is approximately 140,000. So 1,000,000 - 899 + 140,000 = 1,140,000 - 899 = 1,139,101. Wait, that's not matching either. Hmm, maybe I need to use a calculator here. But since I don't have a calculator, let me try to figure out where I went wrong.\n\nWait, in the first method, adding each column:\n\nUnits: 1 + 8 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 4 + 5 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 8 + 7 = 15. So 5, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19. So 9, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. So 3, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo the digits from hundred-thousands to units would be 1 (hundred), 1 (ten), 3 (thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 6 (hundred thousand), 9 (unit). Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place is the sixth digit from the right. So the digits are:\n\nPosition 6: 1 (hundred-thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 1 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 4: 3 (thousands)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 2: 6 (tens)\n\nPosition 1: 9 (units)\n\nSo the number is 113,699. But when I added step by step, I got 1,131,599. Which is 113,1599? Wait, no. Wait, 1,131,599 is 1 million 131 thousand 599. But 113,699 is 113 thousand 699. These are two different numbers. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the alignment when writing the numbers. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n991,841\n\n139,758\n\nSo aligning them:\n\n      9 9 1 8 4 1\n\n    + 1 3 9 7 5 8\n\n------------------\n\nSo the rightmost digits are units, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands, hundred-thousands.\n\nSo:\n\nUnits: 1 + 8 = 9\n\nTens: 4 + 5 = 9\n\nHundreds: 8 + 7 = 15 (5, carry 1)\n\nThousands: 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19 (9, carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13 (3, carry 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11\n\nSo the sum is 11 (hundred-thousands), 13 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (units). Wait, but how does that translate? Let me write the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 9 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 9 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 9 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 3 (ten-thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 1 (hundred-thousands)\n\nSo the number is 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 6 (ten), 9 (unit). Wait, that would be 113,699. But when I added step by step, I got 1,131,599. There's a conflict here.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the step-by-step addition. Let me check again:\n\nStarting with 991,841 + 139,758.\n\nFirst, 991,841 + 100,000 = 1,091,841\n\nThen, 1,091,841 + 30,000 = 1,121,841\n\n1,121,841 + 9,000 = 1,130,841\n\n1,130,841 + 700 = 1,131,541\n\n1,131,541 + 50 = 1,131,591\n\n1,131,591 + 8 = 1,131,599\n\nBut according to the column addition, it's 113,699. Which is 113 thousand vs. 1 million. So there's a discrepancy of 1,116,399. That's a huge difference. So clearly, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment in the column addition. Let me check the numbers again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n991,841\n\n139,758\n\nSo 991,841 is:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9\n\n- Ten thousands: 9\n\n- Thousands: 1\n\n- Hundreds: 8\n\n- Tens: 4\n\n- Units: 1\n\nWait, no. Wait, 991,841:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- The first digit from the left is 9 (hundred thousands place)\n\n- Second digit: 9 (ten thousands)\n\n- Third digit: 1 (thousands)\n\n- Fourth digit: 8 (hundreds)\n\n- Fifth digit: 4 (tens)\n\n- Sixth digit: 1 (units)\n\nSimilarly, 139,758:\n\n- First digit: 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n- Second digit: 3 (ten thousands)\n\n- Third digit: 9 (thousands)\n\n- Fourth digit: 7 (hundreds)\n\n- Fifth digit: 5 (tens)\n\n- Sixth digit: 8 (units)\n\nSo when adding, we need to align them properly:\n\n        9 9 1 8 4 1\n\n      + 1 3 9 7 5 8\n\n-----------------------\n\nSo:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 1 + 8 = 9\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4 + 5 = 9\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 8 + 7 = 15 (5, carry 1)\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 9 + 9 = 18 + 1 = 19 (9, carry 1)\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13 (3, carry 1)\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11\n\nSo the sum is 11 (hundred thousands), 13 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (units). Wait, but how does that translate to the number? Let's