ons), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 8\n\nThousands: 4\n\nHundreds: 8\n\nTens: 1\n\nOnes: 0\n\nSo the number is 1,384,810. Wait, how?\n\nWait, 1 (million) is 1,000,000\n\n3 (hundred thousand) is 300,000\n\n8 (ten thousand) is 80,000\n\n4 (thousand) is 4,000\n\n8 (hundred) is 800\n\n1 (ten) is 10\n\n0 (one) is 0\n\nAdding all together: 1,000,000 + 300,000 = 1,300,000\n\n1,300,000 + 80,000 = 1,380,000\n\n1,380,000 + 4,000 = 1,384,000\n\n1,384,000 + 800 = 1,384,800\n\n1,384,800 + 10 = 1,384,810\n\n1,384,810 + 0 = 1,384,810.\n\nAh! So in the vertical addition, I must have miscounted the digits. Wait, when I wrote the digits from left to right as 1, 3, 8, 4, 7, 1, 0, that's incorrect. Because the hundred thousands place is 3, ten thousands is 8, thousands is 4, hundreds is 8, tens is 1, ones is 0. So the number is 1 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 8 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 1 (ten), 0 (one). So 1,384,810. Which matches the calculator result.\n\nSo where did I go wrong earlier? When I first did the vertical addition, I thought the hundreds place was 7, but actually, the hundreds place is 8. Wait, no. Wait, let's check again.\n\nWait, in the vertical addition:\n\nFirst number: 816,730\n\nSecond number: 568,080\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nOnes: 0 + 0 = 0\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 7 + 0 + 1 = 8\n\nThousands: 6 + 8 = 14 (write 4, carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 1 + 6 + 1 = 8\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 5 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\nMillions: 1\n\nSo the digits from left to right (millions to ones) are:\n\n1, 3, 8, 4, 8, 1, 0\n\nWait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place is 3, ten-thousands is 8, thousands is 4, hundreds is 8, tens is 1, ones is 0. So the number is 1 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 8 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 1 (ten), 0 (one). So 1,384,810. Which matches the