      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of two numbers: 568,393 and 947,514. Hmm, adding them together. Alright, let me start by writing them down one above the other, aligning the digits properly. That usually helps avoid mistakes.\n\nSo, 568,393\n+947,514\n________\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 3 and 4. 3 plus 4 is 7. That's straightforward. I'll write down 7 in the ones place.\n\nNow, moving to the tens place. The digits are 9 and 1. 9 plus 1 is 10. Hmm, that's 10. So I need to carry over 1 to the next column. The result here is 0, and I have a carry of 1. I'll note that 0 in the tens place and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the hundreds place. The digits are 3 and 5. 3 plus 5 is 8. Adding the carryover 1 makes it 9. So, 9 in the hundreds place. No carryover this time.\n\nMoving to the thousands place. The digits are 8 and 7. 8 plus 7 is 15. That's 15. So, 5 in the thousands place with a carryover of 1. Wait, 15 divided by 10 is 1 with a remainder of 5. So, yes, 5 and carryover 1.\n\nNow, the ten-thousands place. The digits are 6 and 4. 6 plus 4 is 10. Again, 0 in the ten-thousands place and carryover 1. So, 0 and carryover 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The digits are 5 and 9. 5 plus 9 is 14. Adding the carryover 1 makes it 15. So, 5 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1. Wait, 15 divided by 10 is 1 with a remainder of 5. So, 5 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 will be added as a new digit in the millions place. So, 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits we have are:\n\nMillions place: 1\n\nHundred-thousands place: 5\n\nTen-thousands place: 0\n\nThousands place: 5\n\nHundreds place: 9\n\nTens place: 0\n\nOnes place: 7\n\nSo, combining those, the sum should be 1,505,907. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, let me verify step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3 + 4 = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10. Carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 8 + 7 = 15. Carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 6 + 4 = 10. Carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 5 + 9 = 14. Carryover 1. Then add the carryover to get 15. So 5 in hundred-thousands and 1 in millions. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo the total is 1,505,907. Let me do a quick check by adding the numbers another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers:\n\n568,393 can be thought of as 500,000 + 60,000 + 8,000 + 300 + 90 + 3.\n\n947,514 is 900,000 + 40,000 + 7,000 + 500 + 10 + 4.\n\nAdding the hundred thousands: 500,000 + 900,000 = 1,400,000.\n\nAdding the ten thousands: 60,000 + 40,000 = 100,000.\n\nThousands: 8,000 + 7,000 = 15,000.\n\nHundreds: 300 + 500 = 800.\n\nTens: 90 + 10 = 100.\n\nOnes: 3 + 4 = 7.\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n1,400,000 + 100,000 = 1,500,000\n\n1,500,000 + 15,000 = 1,515,000\n\n1,515,000 + 800 = 1,515,800\n\n1,515,800 + 100 = 1,515,900\n\n1,515,900 + 7 = 1,515,907.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Wait, no, 1,515,907. Wait, but earlier I had 1,505,907. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check again. Wait, in the first method, I had:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 5\n\nTen-thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 5\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 7\n\nSo 1,505,907.\n\nBut in the second method, adding each category, I ended up with 1,515,907.\n\nWait, so which is correct? Let me check the second method again.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n568,393:\n\n500,000 + 60,000 + 8,000 + 300 + 90 + 3.\n\n947,514:\n\n900,000 + 40,000 + 7,000 + 500 + 10 + 4.\n\nAdding:\n\n500,000 + 900,000 = 1,400,000\n\n60,000 + 40,000 = 100,000\n\n8,000 + 7,000 = 15,000\n\n300 + 500 = 800\n\n90 + 10 = 100\n\n3 + 4 = 7\n\nNow adding all these:\n\n1,400,000 + 100,000 = 1,500,000\n\n1,500,000 + 15,000 = 1,515,000\n\n1,515,000 + 800 = 1,515,800\n\n1,515,800 + 100 = 1,515,900\n\n1,515,900 + 7 = 1,515,907.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total is 1,515,907. But in the first method, I got 1,505,907. There's a difference of 100,000. That's a problem. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check the first method again.\n\nFirst method:\n\n568,393\n\n+947,514\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 + 4 = 7\n\n9 + 1 = 10, carryover 1\n\n3 + 5 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\n8 + 7 = 15, carryover 1\n\n6 + 4 = 10, carryover 1\n\n5 + 9 = 14 + 1 = 15, carryover 1. Then add that as a new digit.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 7 (ones). So 1,505,907.\n\nBut according to the second method, it's 1,515,907. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the second method. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, in the second method, when adding the ten thousands: 60,000 + 40,000 = 100,000. That's correct.\n\nThousands: 8,000 + 7,000 = 15,000. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 300 + 500 = 800. Correct.\n\nTens: 90 + 10 = 100. Correct.\n\nOnes: 3 + 4 = 7. Correct.\n\nAdding all these:\n\n1,400,000 (hundred thousands) + 100,000 (ten thousands) = 1,500,000\n\n1,500,000 + 15,000 (thousands) = 1,515,000\n\n1,515,000 + 800 (hundreds) = 1,515,800\n\n1,515,800 + 100 (tens) = 1,515,900\n\n1,515,900 + 7 (ones) = 1,515,907.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total is 1,515,907. But according to the first method, it's 1,505,907. There's a discrepancy of 100,000. That's a big difference. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst method:\n\n568,393\n\n+947,514\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3 + 4 = 7. Correct.\n\nTens: 9 + 1 = 10. Carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 = 9. Correct.\n\nThousands: 8 + 7 = 15. Carryover 1. So 5 in thousands place and 1 carried over. Wait, 15 divided by 10 is 1, remainder 5. So thousands place is 5, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousan