rryover 1 to the next position. Wait, but in this case, the hundred thousands place is the highest, so we just add 1 to the hundred thousands place.\n\nWait, but the first number's hundred thousands digit is 8, the second number's hundred thousands digit is 2. So 8 + 2 = 10, plus the carryover 1 = 11. So we write down 1 and carryover 1. But there are no more digits, so the carryover 1 becomes a new digit in the hundred thousands place. Wait, but the first number is only six digits. So adding 802,866 (six digits) and 299,071 (six digits) gives a number that could be seven digits. Let's check:\n\n802,866 + 299,071. Let's compute 802,866 + 200,000 = 1,002,866\n\n1,002,866 + 99,000 = 1,001,866\n\n1,001,866 + 71 = 1,001,937\n\nWait, so that's 1,001,937. But according to the column addition, we had:\n\nHundred thousands: 1\n\nTen thousands: 0\n\nThousands: 2\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 3\n\nOnes: 7\n\nSo 102,937. Wait, that's conflicting. There's a mistake here. Wait, no. Wait, 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones). So 102,937. But that's only six digits. But 802,866 + 299,071 should be more than a million. Wait, 802,866 is 802 thousand something, and 299,071 is 299 thousand something. So total is 1,001,937. So why is the column addition giving 102,937?\n\nWait, clearly, there's a mistake in the column addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me write the numbers vertically again:\n\n      802866\n\n    + 299071\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 + 1 = 7 (ones place)\n\n6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1 (tens place)\n\n8 + 0 = 8 + 1 = 9 (hundreds place)\n\n2 + 9 = 11 \u2192 11, carryover 1 (thousands place)\n\n0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1 (ten thousands place)\n\n8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 11, carryover 1 (hundred thousands place)\n\nBut since there are no more digits, we add the carryover 1 to the hundred thousands place. So the hundred thousands place becomes 8 + 2 + 1 = 11. So we write down 1 and carryover 1. But there's no higher place, so we have to add that 1 as a new digit. So the total is 1 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 3 (hundred), 7 (ten), 7 (one). Wait, no. Wait, the carryover 1 from the hundred thousands place would make the hundred thousands digit 8 + 2 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. But the first number's hundred thousands digit is 8, the second is 2. So 8 + 2 = 10, plus carryover 1 = 11. So we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. So the millions place is 1. So the total is 1 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 3 (hundred), 7 (ten), 7 (one). So 1,029,377? Wait, but that contradicts the previous result of 1,001,937.\n\nWait, now I'm confused. Let me check with another method. Let's compute 802,866 + 299,071.\n\nLet me compute 802,866 + 299,071:\n\nFirst, add 802,866 + 200,000 = 1,002,866\n\nThen add 99,071 to 1,002,866:\n\n1,002,866 + 99,071\n\nBreak down 99,071 into 90,000 + 9,000 + 71.\n\n1,002,866 + 90,000 = 1,092,866\n\n1,092,866 + 9,000 = 1,101,866\n\n1,101,866 + 71 = 1,101,937\n\nSo the correct sum is 1,101,937. But according to the column addition, we were getting 102,937. That's way off. So there's a mistake in the column addition process. Let me figure out where.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me write the numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 802,866\n\nSecond number: 299,071\n\nSo aligning:\n\n      802866\n\n    + 299071\n\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6 + 1 = 7\n\nTens: 6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 8 + 0 = 8 + 1 = 9\n\nThousands: 2 + 9 = 11 \u2192 11, carryover 1\n\nTen-thousands: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 11, carryover 1\n\nBut since there are no more digits, we have to add the carryover 1 to the hundred-