lace is 11. Wait, but 11 in the hundred-thousands place would mean 11 hundred thousands, which is 1,100,000. But adding that to the previous 1,002,866 gives 1,102,866. Then adding 929 gives 1,102,866 - 929 = 1,101,937. So where is the mistake in the column addition?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The hundred-thousands place in the first number is 8, and in the second number, there is no digit in the hundred-thousands place. So when adding, the first number's hundred-thousands digit is 8, and the second number's hundred-thousands digit is 0. So 8 + 0 = 8, plus any carryover from the ten-thousands place. Wait, but earlier, we had a carryover from the ten-thousands place. Wait, let's re-examine the steps:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 1 = 7. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens: 6 + 7 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 0 = 8 + 1 = 9. No carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 11, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11, carryover 1.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, we add the carryover 1 to the millions place. So the total is 1 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 0 (ten-thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place is part of the carryover. Wait, no. Wait, the hundred-thousands place in the first number is 8, and the second number has no digit there. So when adding, it's 8 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 8, plus any carryover from the ten-thousands place. Wait, but in step 5, we had a carryover from the ten-thousands place addition. Let me retrace:\n\nWait, step-by-step:\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 1 = 7. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens: 6 + 7 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 8 + 0 = 8 + 1 = 9. No carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 11, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11, carryover 1.\n\nNow, s