 hundreds\n\n1 ten\n\n5 ones. So that's correct.\n\nSo when I broke them down:\n\n100,000 + 500,000 = 600,000\n\n18,000 + 16,000 = 34,000\n\n700 + 600 = 1,300\n\n30 + 10 = 40\n\n2 + 5 = 7\n\nAdding those gives 600,000 + 34,000 = 634,000; 634,000 + 1,300 = 635,300; 635,300 + 40 = 635,340; 635,340 +7 = 635,347. But when I added digit by digit, I got 675,347. That's a problem. There's a mistake here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the digit-by-digit addition again.\n\nFirst number: 118,732\n\nSecond number: 516,615\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2 + 5 = 7. Correct.\n\nTens: 3 + 1 = 4. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 7 + 6 = 13. So 3, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nThousands: 8 + 1 = 9, plus 1 carried over = 10. So 0, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTen thousands: 1 + 5 = 6, plus 1 carried over = 7. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands: 1 + 5 = 6. Correct.\n\nSo the sum is 675,347. But when breaking down, I got 635,347. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the breakdown. Let me check the breakdown again.\n\nFirst number: 118,732.\n\n1 hundred thousand: 100,000\n\n1 ten thousand: 10,000\n\n8 thousands: 8,000\n\n7 hundreds: 700\n\n3 tens: 30\n\n2 ones: 2\n\nSo adding those: 100,000 + 10,000 = 110,000\n\n110,000 + 8,000 = 118,000\n\n118,000 + 700 = 118,700\n\n118,700 + 30 = 118,730\n\n118,730 + 2 = 118,732. Correct.\n\nSecond number: 516,615.\n\n5 hundred thousands: 500,000\n\n1 ten thousand: 10,000\n\n6 thousands: 6,000\n\n6 hundreds: 600\n\n1 ten: 10\n\n5 ones: 5\n\nAdding those: 500,000 + 10,000 = 510,000\n\n510,000 + 6,000 = 516,000\n\n516,000 + 600 = 516,600\n\n516,600 + 10 = 516,610\n\n516,610 + 5 = 516,615. Correct.\n\nAdding 118,732 + 516,615:\n\n118,732 + 516,615. Let's do this as 118,732 + 500,000 = 618,732\n\n618,732 + 16,000 = 634,732\n\n634,732 + 600 = 635,332\n\n635,332 + 10 = 635,342\n\n635,342 + 5 = 635,347. Wait, now this is different. Wait, so according to this step-by-step addition, starting from 118,732 and adding 500,000