ace. So the hundred-thousands digit is 1, and the ten-thousands digit becomes 11 (from 1 + 2). Wait, no. Wait, let's reconstruct the entire number.\n\nWait, maybe writing it out step by step:\n\nStart with the ones place:\n\n3 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 7. Write 7.\n\nTens place:\n\n6 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n9 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 11. Add the carryover 1: 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 7. Add carryover 1: 8. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n1 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 3. Add carryover 0: 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n2 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 11. Add carryover 0: 11. Write 11. So that's 1 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 in the ten-thousands place.\n\nWait, so combining all the digits:\n\nHundred-thousands: 1\n\nTen-thousands: 1\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 2\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 7\n\nSo the total is 111,8207? Wait, no. Wait, when you write the digits, it's 1 (hundred-thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 7 (ones). So 111,8207? Wait, that can't be. Wait, 111,8207 would be 111,8207, but commas are placed every three digits. So 111,8207 would be 1118207. But that seems too high. Wait, but 212,963 + 925,244. Let's check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding the numbers as 212,963 + 925,244. Let's compute 212,963 + 900,000 = 1,112,963. Then add 25,244: 1,112,963 + 25,244. \n\n1,112,963 + 20,000 = 1,132,963\n\n1,132,963 + 5,000 = 1,137,963\n\n1,137,963 + 200 = 1,138,163\n\n1,138,163 + 44 = 1,138,207\n\nWait, so that gives 1,138,207. But according to the previous step-by-step addition, I had 111,8207 which is 111,8207. Wait, that's conflicting. So which is correct?\n\nWait, clearly, there's a mistake here. Let me check the second method again. \n\nOriginal numbers: 212,963 and 925,244.\n\nAdding 212,963 + 925,244.\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n212,963 + 900,000 = 1,112,963\n\nThen, 1,112,963 + 25,244. Let's compute 1,112,963 + 25,244.\n\n1,112,963 + 20,000 = 1,132,963\n\n1,132,963 + 5,000 = 1,137,963\n\n1,137,963 + 200 = 1,138,163\n\n1,138,163 + 44 = 1,138,207\n\nSo total is 1,138,207. But according to the vertical addition, I had 111,8207. That's a big difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let's do the vertical addition again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 212,963\n\nSecond number: 925,244\n\nAligning:\n\n      212,963\n    + 925,244\n    ----------\n      ?\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\n3 + 4 = 7. Write 7.\n\nNext (tens place):\n\n6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNext (hundreds place):\n\n9 + 2 = 11. Add carryover 1: 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nNext (thousands place):\n\n2 + 5 = 7. Add carryover 1: 8. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nNext (ten-thousands place):\n\n1 + 2 = 3. Add carryover 0: 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nNext (hundred-thousands place):\n\n2 + 9 = 11. Add carryover 0: 11. Write 11.\n\nSo the result is 11 (hundred-thousands and ten-thousands place) 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, but how does that translate into the number? Let's see:\n\nHundred-thousands: 11 (which is 1 carried over to the next place, but since it's the highest place, it's just 11 in the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands positions)\n\nWait, no. Wait, when adding the hundred-thousands place:\n\nFirst number's hundred-thousands digit is 2, second number's hundred-thousands digit is 9. 2 + 9 = 11. So we write down 11. So the hundred-thousands digit is 1 and the ten-thousands digit is 1. Then the thousands digit is 8, hundreds is 2, tens is 0, ones is 7. So the number is 111,8207? Wait, that can't be. Wait, 11 in the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands place would make it 110,000 + 10,000 = 120,000. Then adding 8,207 gives 120,000 + 8,207 = 128,207. Wait, but that's not matching the second method whi