 + 900,000 = 1,100,000\n\nThen, add 12,963 + 25,244.\n\n12,963 + 25,244:\n\n12,963 + 20,000 = 32,963\n\n32,963 + 5,000 = 37,963\n\n37,963 + 200 = 38,163\n\n38,163 + 44 = 38,207\n\nSo total is 1,100,000 + 38,207 = 1,138,207.\n\nBut according to the vertical addition, I got 148,207. That's a huge discrepancy. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let's check the vertical addition again. Maybe I messed up the digits.\n\nWait, the first number is 212,963. So:\n\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 1 (ten thousands)\n\n- 2 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n- 6 (tens)\n\n- 3 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 925,244\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 2 (ten thousands)\n\n- 5 (thousands)\n\n- 2 (hundreds)\n\n- 4 (tens)\n\n- 4 (ones)\n\nAdding them:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 2 + 9 = 11. So 11. Then ten thousands: 1 + 2 = 3. Then thousands: 2 + 5 = 7. Hundreds: 9 + 2 = 11. Tens: 6 + 4 = 10. Ones: 3 + 4 = 7.\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the 11 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place would make it 11 followed by 3, then 7, then 11 in the hundreds and tens place? Wait, no. Wait, the places are:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 11\n\n- Ten thousands: 3\n\n- Thousands: 7\n\n- Hundreds: 11\n\n- Tens: 10\n\n- Ones: 7\n\nBut how do you write that as a number? The hundred thousands and ten thousands place: 11 and 3. So 11 (hundred thousands) and 3 (ten thousands) would be 11*10,000 + 3*10,000 = 110,000 + 30,000 = 140,000.\n\nThen thousands: 7*1,000 = 7,000.\n\nHundreds: 11*100 = 1,100.\n\nTens: 10*10 = 100.\n\nOnes: 7*1 = 7.\n\nAdding all together: 140,000 + 7,000 = 147,000; 147,000 + 1,100 = 148,100; 148,100 + 100 = 148,200; 148,200 + 7 = 148,207.\n\nBut according to the other method, the sum is 1,138,207. So there's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 212,963 + 925,244.\n\n212,963 + 925,244:\n\nLet's compute 212,963 + 925,244.\n\n212,963 + 900,000 = 1,112,963\n\n1,112,963 + 25,244 = ?\n\n1,112,963 + 25,244:\n\n1,112,963 + 20,000 = 1,132,96