n- 4: tens\n- 5: units\n\nFor 448,856:\n\n- 4: hundred thousands\n- 4: ten thousands\n- 8: thousands\n- 8: hundreds\n- 5: tens\n- 6: units\n\nSo when adding, starting from the right:\n\nUnits: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTens: 4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nThousands: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nBut wait, the hundred-thousands place in the second number is 4, so adding 8 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 12, plus the carried 1 is 13. So the result is 3 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 carried over. But since there are no more digits, we just add that carried 1 at the beginning. Wait, but in the hundred-thousands place, the first number is 8 and the second is 4, so 8 + 4 = 12, plus the carried 1 is 13. So that would mean the hundred-thousands digit is 3 and we have a carryover of 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 would be added as a new digit at the beginning. Wait, but in the original numbers, both are six-digit numbers. Adding two six-digit numbers can result in a seven-digit number. Let's check.\n\nWait, 879,245 is approximately 880,000 and 448,856 is approximately 450,000. So adding them together, the sum should be around 1,330,000. So 1,330,000-something. Let's see if that matches with our calculation.\n\nWait, according to our column addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 1\n\nTens: 0\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nThousands: 8\n\nTen-thousands: 2\n\nHundred-thousands: 3\n\nSo putting it all together: 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units). So the total is 328,101. Wait, but that can't be right because 879,245 + 448,856 should be more than 879,245. Wait, 879,245 + 400,000 is 1,279,245. Then adding 48,856 more: 1,279,245 + 48,856 = 1,328,101. So the sum should be 1,328,101. But according to my column addition, I got 328,101. That's a discrepancy. So I must have made a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's redo the column addition step by step carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n      879,245\n    + 448,856\n    -----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n5 (from 879,245) + 6 (from 448,856) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nNext, tens place:\n\n4 (from 879,245) + 5 (from 448,856) = 9. Add the carried 1: 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n2 (from 879,245) + 8 (from 448,856) = 10. Add carried 1: 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n9 (from 879,245) + 8 (from 448,856) = 17. Add carried 1: 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n7 (from 879,245) + 4 (from 448,856) = 11. Add carried 1: 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n8 (from 879,245) + 4 (from 448,856) = 12. Add carried 1: 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, we add the carried 1 as a new digit at the beginning. So the total is 1,328,101. Wait, but in the hundred-thousands place, we have 3, but the carried 1 is added as a hundred-thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, when we add the hundred-thousands place digits (8 and 4), we get 12, plus the carried 1 is 13. So that 13 would mean 3 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 carried over. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit in the millions place. Wait, but 879,245 is 8 hundred thousands, 7 ten thousands, etc. 448,856 is 4 hundred thousands, 4 ten thousands, etc. So adding 8 + 4 = 12 hundred thousands, plus the carried 1 makes 13 hundred thousands. So 13 hundred thousands is 1,300,000. Then the rest is 28,101. So total is 1,328,101. But according to the column addition, we have 3 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 carried over. Wait, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let's list the digits with their places again:\n\nFirst number: 879,245\n\nDigits: 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 5 (units)\n\nSecond number: 448,856\n\nDigits: 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (units)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nUnits: 5 + 6 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nTens: 4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\nThousands: 9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18 \u2192 8, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nBut since there's no digit beyond the hundred-thousands place, the carried 1 becomes a new digit in the millions place. So the total is 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units). So 1,328,101. \n\nBut when I first thought, adding 879,245 + 448,856, I approximated 880,000 + 450,000 = 1,330,000, and the exact difference would be 1,330,000 - (1,755) = 1,328,245? Wait, no. Wait, 879,245 + 448,856. Let me compute it step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's compute 879,245 + 448,856.\n\nBreak down the numbers:\n\n879,245 = 800,000 + 70,000 + 9,000 + 200 + 40 +5\n\n448,856 = 400,000 + 40,000 + 8,000 + 800 + 50 +6\n\nAdding them together:\n\n800,000 + 400,000 = 1,200,000\n\n70,000 + 40,000 = 110,000\n\n9,000 + 8,000 = 17,000\n\n200 + 800 = 1,000\n\n40 + 50 = 90\n\n5 + 6 = 11\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n1,200,000 + 110,000 = 1,310,000\n\n1,310,000 + 17,000 = 1,327,000\n\n1,327,000 + 1,000 = 1,328,000\n\n1,328,000 + 90 = 1,328,090\n\n1,328,090 + 11 = 1,328,101\n\nYes, that's 1,328,101. So the column addition must have gone wrong earlier. Wait, why? Let me check again.\n\nWait, in the column addition, when adding the hundred-thousands place: 8 + 4 = 12, plus the carried 1 is 13. So that 13 would be 3 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 carried over. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit in the millions place. So the total is 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units). So 1,328,101. Which matches the breakdown method. So where did I go wrong in the initial column addition?\n\nAh, I see. When I first did the column addition, I thought the hundred-thousands place was 3, but actually, the carried 1 from the ten-thousands place addition (which was 12) plus the carried 1 from the hundreds place addition (which was 11 +1=12? Wait, no. Wait, let's retrace:\n\nWait, the ten-thousands place addition was 7 + 4 = 11, plus the carried 1 from the hundreds place (which was 11 +1=12). Wait, no. Wait, the hundreds place addition was 2 +8=10, plus carried 1=11. Then thousands place 9 +8=17, plus carried 1=18. Then tens place 4 +5=9, plus carried 1=10. Units place 5 +6=11, plus carried 1=12. Wait, no. Wait, no, the order is important. Let me do it step by step again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the order of the columns. Let me write the numbers vertically with the correct alignment:\n\n        879,245\n      + 448,856\n      -----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n5 (from 879,245) + 6 (from 448,856) = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nTens place:\n\n4 (from 879,245) + 5 (from 448,856) = 9. Add carried 1: 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n2 (from 879,245) + 8 (from 448,856) = 10. Add carried 1: 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n9 (from 879,245) + 8 (from 448,856) = 17. Add carried 1: 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n7 (from 879,245) + 4 (from 448,856) = 11. Add carried 1: 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n8 (from 879,245) + 4 (from 448,856) = 12. Add carried 1: 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nSince there are no more columns, add the carried 1 as a new digit in the millions place. So the total is 1,328,101. \n\nYes, that's correct. So my initial mistake was in the hundred-thousands place. I thought the carried 1 from the ten-thousands place addition (which was 12) plus the carried 1 from the hundreds place addition (which was 11 +1=12?) Wait, no. Wait, the hundreds place addition was 2 +8=10, plus carried 1=11. Then thousands place 9 +8=17, plus carried 1=18. Then ten-thousands place 7 +4=11, plus carried 1=12. Then hundred-thousands place 8 +4=12, plus carried 1=13. So the 13 is in the hundred-thousands place, and the carried 1 is added as a millions place. So the total is 1,328,101. \n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 1,328,101. \n\nAlternatively, using another method: 879,245 + 448,856. Let's add 879,245 + 400,000 = 1,279,245. Then add 48,856 to that. 1,279,245 + 40,000 = 1,319,245. Then add 8,856: 1,319,245 + 8,856 = 1,328,101. \n\nYes, that's the same result. So the answer is 1,328,101. \n\nBut let me check once more with another approach. Let's use the standard addition algorithm:\n\n          879,245\n        + 448,856\n        -----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 + 6 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\n2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\n9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\n7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nAdd the carried 1 as a new digit: 1.\n\nSo the total is 1,328,101. \n\nYes, that's consistent. Therefore, the sum of 87,9245 and 448,856 is 1,328,101. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe sum of 879245 and 448856 is \\boxed{1328101}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the sum of 87,9245 and 448,856, we align the numbers vertically and add each column starting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  \\phantom{0}879245 \\\\\n+ \\phantom{0}448856 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nStarting from the units place:\n- \\(5 + 6 = 11\\). Write down 1, carry over 1.\n- \\(4 + 5 = 9 + 1 = 10\\). Write down 0, carry over 1.\n- \\(2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11\\). Write down 1, carry over 1.\n- \\(9 + 8 = 17 + 1 = 18\\). Write down 8, carry over 1.\n- \\(7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12\\). Write down 2, carry over 1.\n- \\(8 + 4 = 12 + 1 = 13\\). Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nSince there are no more digits, add the carried over 1 as a new digit in the millions place.\n\nThe final result is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1328101}\n\\]",
