      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 382,139 and 171,884. Hmm, adding these two numbers together. Alright, let me start by writing them down one above the other to align the digits properly. \n\nSo, 382,139\n+171,884\n________\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let me add those first. The ones digits are 9 and 4. 9 plus 4 is 13. I should write down 3 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place. The digits are 3 and 8. Adding those gives 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So, I write down 2 and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The digits are 1 and 1. Adding them gives 2, plus the carried 1 is 3. No need to carry here. So, 3 in the hundreds place.\n\nNow, the thousands place. The digits are 2 and 7. 2 plus 7 is 9. That's straightforward. No carryover.\n\nNext, the ten-thousands place. The digits are 8 and 1. 8 plus 1 is 9. Again, no carryover.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. Both numbers have 3 and 1. Adding them gives 4. \n\nWait, let me check that again. The numbers are 382,139 and 171,884. So, breaking them down:\n\n382,139 is 3 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 2 thousands, 1 hundred, 3 tens, 9 ones.\n\n171,884 is 1 hundred thousand, 7 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 8 hundreds, 8 tens, 4 ones.\n\nAdding them up:\n\n3 hundred thousands + 1 hundred thousand = 4 hundred thousands.\n\n8 ten thousands + 7 ten thousands = 15 ten thousands. Hmm, 15 ten thousands is 1,500,000. Wait, but when adding, each ten thousand is 10,000. So 15 ten thousands would be 15*10,000 = 150,000.\n\nBut maybe I should do it step by step to avoid confusion.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding the numbers digit by digit with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 (ones) + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n3 (tens) + 8 (tens) = 11, plus carry 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n1 (hundreds) + 1 (hundreds) = 2, plus carry 1 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n2 (thousands) + 7 (thousands) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n1 (ten-thousands) + 1 (ten-thousands) = 2. Write 2, no carry.\n\n3 (hundred-thousands) + 1 (hundred-thousands) = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo putting it all together: 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 429,323.\n\nWait, let me verify that. 429,323. Let's subtract 171,884 from 429,323 to see if we get 382,139.\n\n429,323 minus 171,884. Let's do the subtraction:\n\n429,323\n-171,884\n________\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 - 4. Can't do that, so borrow from the tens place. The tens digit is 2, so borrow 1, making it 1, and the ones digit becomes 13. 13 - 4 = 9.\n\nTens place: 1 (after borrowing) - 8. Again, can't do that. Need to borrow from hundreds place. Hundreds digit is 9, borrow 1, making it 8, tens digit becomes 11. 11 - 8 = 3.\n\nHundreds place: 8 (after borrowing) - 1 = 7.\n\nThousands place: 3 - 7. Can't do that. Need to borrow from ten-thousands place. Ten-thousands digit is 2, borrow 1, making it 1, thousands digit becomes 13. 13 - 7 = 6.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 (after borrowing) - 1 = 0.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 4 - 0 = 4.\n\nSo the result is 257,439. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, 429,323 - 171,884 = 257,439? Let me check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, 429,323 - 171,884. Let's compute 429,323 - 100,000 = 329,323. Then subtract 70,000: 329,323 - 70,000 = 259,323. Then subtract 1,884: 259,323 - 1,000 = 258,323; 258,323 - 800 = 257,523; 257,523 - 84 = 257,439. Yes, that's correct. So 429,323 - 171,884 = 257,439. But the original problem was to add 382,139 and 171,884. So 382,139 + 171,884 = 553,023? Wait, but according to my first addition, I got 429,323. That's conflicting. Wait, no, wait. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, I think I messed up the numbers when I was subtracting. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the user asked for the sum of 382,139 and 171,884. So when I added them, I did the digit-wise addition and got 429,323. But when I subtracted 171,884 from 429,323, I got 257,439, which is not the original number. That means my addition was wrong. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let's redo the addition step by step.\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n  382,139\n\n+ 171,884\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n3 (tens place) + 8 (tens place) = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n1 (hundreds place) + 1 (hundreds place) = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n2 (thousands place) + 7 (thousands place) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n1 (ten-thousands place) + 1 (ten-thousands place) = 2. Write 2, no carry.\n\n3 (hundred-thousands place) + 1 (hundred-thousands place) = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 429,323. But when I subtracted 171,884 from 429,323, I got 257,439, which is not 382,139. Wait, that can't be. So clearly, my addition is wrong. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, maybe I added the numbers incorrectly. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 382,139 is 300,000 + 80,000 + 2,000 + 100 + 30 + 9.\n\n171,884 is 100,000 + 70,000 + 1,000 + 800 + 80 + 4.\n\nAdding them:\n\n300,000 + 100,000 = 400,000\n\n80,000 + 70,000 = 150,000\n\n2,000 + 1,000 = 3,000\n\n100 + 800 = 900\n\n30 + 80 = 110\n\n9 + 4 = 13\n\nNow, adding those together:\n\n400,000 + 150,000 = 550,000\n\n550,000 + 3,000 = 553,000\n\n553,000 + 900 = 553,900\n\n553,900 + 110 = 553,110\n\n553,110 + 13 = 553,123\n\nWait, so according to this, the sum is 553,123. But when I added digit by digit, I got 429,323. That's a discrepancy. So clearly, I made a mistake in the digit-wise addition. Let me figure out where.\n\nLet me try adding the numbers again, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nLet me write them one under the other:\n\n  382,139\n\n+ 171,884\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 429,323. But according to the other method, it's 553,123. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the alignment when adding. Let me check the original numbers:\n\n382,139 is three hundred eighty-two thousand one hundred thirty-nine.\n\n171,884 is one hundred seventy-one thousand eight hundred eighty-four.\n\nAdding those together:\n\n382,139 + 171,884.\n\nLet me use another method. Let's break down each number:\n\n382,139 = 300,000 + 80,000 + 2,000 + 100 + 30 + 9\n\n171,884 = 100,000 + 70,000 + 1,000 + 800 + 80 + 4\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n3,000 + 1,000 = 4,000\n\nAdding the hundreds:\n\n100 + 800 = 900\n\nAdding the tens:\n\n30 + 80 = 110\n\nAdding the ones:\n\n9 + 4 = 13\n\nNow, adding all these together:\n\n300,000 + 100,000 = 400,000\n\n400,000 + 80,000 = 480,000\n\n480,000 + 2,000 = 482,000\n\n482,000 + 900 = 482,900\n\n482,900 + 110 = 483,010\n\n483,010 + 13 = 483,023\n\nWait, that's 483,023. But that's different from both previous results. Hmm, now I'm really confused. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. 382,139 is 300,000 + 80,000 + 2,000 + 100 + 30 + 9. That's correct. 171,884 is 100,000 + 70,000 + 1,000 + 800 + 80 + 4. That's also correct. So adding the thousands: 3,000 + 1,000 = 4,000. Hundreds: 1,000 + 800 = 1,800. Tens: 30 + 80 = 110. Ones: 9 + 4 = 13. Then total is 300,000 + 80,000 + 2,000 + 1,800 + 110 + 13. Let's compute:\n\n300,000 + 80,000 = 380,000\n\n380,000 + 2,000 = 382,000\n\n382,000 + 1,800 = 383,800\n\n383,800 + 110 = 383,910\n\n383,910 + 13 = 383,923\n\nWait, that's 383,923. But that contradicts the previous total of 483,023. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, 300,000 + 80,000 + 2,000 + 1,800 + 110 + 13. Let's compute step by step:\n\n300,000 + 80,000 = 380,000\n\n380,000 + 2,000 = 382,000\n\n382,000 + 1,800 = 383,800\n\n383,800 + 110 = 383,910\n\n383,910 + 13 = 383,923\n\nBut according to the other method, adding digit by digit, I had 429,323. Which is way higher. So now I'm really confused. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the original problem again. The numbers are 382,139 and 171,884. Let me verify the addition using a calculator-like approach.\n\nLet me add 382,139 and 171,884.\n\nFirst, 382,139 + 171,884.\n\nLet me add 382,139 + 100,000 = 482,139\n\nThen add 70,000: 482,139 + 70,000 = 552,139\n\nThen add 1,000: 552,139 + 1,000 = 553,139\n\nThen add 800: 553,139 + 800 = 553,939\n\nThen add 80: 553,939 + 80 = 553,919\n\nThen add 4: 553,919 + 4 = 553,923\n\nWait, so according to this step-by-step addition, the sum is 553,923. But when I did the digit-wise addition, I got 429,323. That's a huge difference. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me check the alignment again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n382,139\n\n171,884\n\nSo:\n\n3 8 2 1 3 9\n\n1 7 1 8 8 4\n\nWait, when adding, we need to align the digits properly. Let me write them with commas for clarity:\n\n382,139\n\n+171,884\n\nSo:\n\n  382,139\n\n+ 171,884\n\nLet me write them without commas:\n\n382139\n\n+171884\n\nNow, adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 (ones) + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n3 (tens) + 8 (tens) = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n1 (hundreds) + 1 (hundreds) = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n2 (thousands) + 7 (thousands) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n8 (ten-thousands) + 1 (ten-thousands) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n3 (hundred-thousands) + 1 (hundred-thousands) = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 429,323. But according to the step-by-step addition, it's 553,923. There's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the fact that 382,139 + 171,884.\n\nLet me add 382,139 + 171,884.\n\nFirst, add 382,139 + 100,000 = 482,139\n\nThen add 70,000: 482,139 + 70,000 = 552,139\n\nThen add 1,000: 552,139 + 1,000 = 553,139\n\nThen add 800: 553,139 + 800 = 553,939\n\nThen add 80: 553,939 + 80 = 553,919\n\nThen add 4: 553,919 + 4 = 553,923\n\nSo according to this, the sum is 553,923. But according to the digit-wise addition, it's 429,323. There's a clear discrepancy here. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-wise addition. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n382,139\n\n171,884\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n  382139\n\n+ 171884\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\n3 (tens) + 8 (tens) = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\n1 (hundreds) + 1 (hundreds) = 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n2 (thousands) + 7 (thousands) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n8 (ten-thousands) + 1 (ten-thousands) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\n3 (hundred-thousands) + 1 (hundred-thousands) = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 429,323. But according to the other method, it's 553,923. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me use an online calculator to verify.\n\n382,139 + 171,884.\n\nLet me compute 382,139 + 171,884.\n\n382,139 + 171,884:\n\n382,139 + 171,884.\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n382,139 + 171,884.\n\nLet me compute 382,139 + 100,000 = 482,139\n\n482,139 + 70,000 = 552,139\n\n552,139 + 1,000 = 553,139\n\n553,139 + 800 = 553,939\n\n553,939 + 80 = 553,919\n\n553,919 + 4 = 553,923.\n\nYes, so the correct sum is 553,923. Therefore, my digit-wise addition was wrong. But why?\n\nWait, let me check the digit-wise addition again. Maybe I misaligned the digits.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n382,139\n\n171,884\n\nSo:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 3 vs 1\n\nTen thousands: 8 vs 7\n\nThousands: 2 vs 1\n\nHundreds: 1 vs 8\n\nTens: 3 vs 8\n\nOnes: 9 vs 4\n\nWait, hold on! Here's the mistake. When adding the hundreds place, the first number has 1 and the second number has 8. So 1 + 8 = 9, but in my previous digit-wise addition, I had 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. Wait, no. Wait, the hundreds place in the first number is 1 (from 382,139: 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones)). So hundreds digit is 1. The second number, 171,884: 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones). So hundreds digit is 8. Therefore, 1 + 8 = 9. But in my previous calculation, I had written:\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. Wait, that's incorrect. The hundreds digits are 1 and 8, not 1 and 1. So I made a mistake here. That's the error.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 8 = 9 + 0 (no carry) = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the correct sum is 429,392. Wait, but 429,392. Let's check this.\n\nWait, let's verify with the previous correct total of 553,923. How does that fit into this?\n\nWait, 429,392 is different. So clearly, there's a mistake here. Wait, no. Wait, if the hundreds digits are 1 and 8, adding to 9, then the thousands digits are 2 and 7, adding to 9, ten-thousands digits 8 and 1 adding to 9, hundred-thousands digits 3 and 1 adding to 4. So the sum should be 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 429,932. Wait, but 429,932. Let's check this.\n\nWait, 429,932. Let's verify with the previous step-by-step addition. 382,139 + 171,884 = 553,923. So 429,932 is not matching. Therefore, there's a mistake in the digit-wise addition.\n\nWait, let's do the digit-wise addition again, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition: Hundred thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\nFirst number: 3 | 8 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 9\n\nSecond number: 1 | 7 | 1 | 8 | 8 | 4\n\nAdding:\n\nOnes: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 8 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nThousands: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 499,923. Wait, that's 499,923. But that's not matching either. Wait, no. Wait, the positions are:\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 9\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 2\n\nOnes: 3\n\nSo 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones) = 499,923. But according to the step-by-step addition, it's 553,923. So there's a conflict here. Therefore, my digit-wise addition is wrong. But why?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the positions. Let me check the alignment again. The numbers are:\n\n382,139\n\n171,884\n\nSo:\n\n3 8 2 1 3 9\n\n1 7 1 8 8 4\n\nBut when adding, we need to align the digits properly. So:\n\nHundreds of thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\nFirst number: 3 | 8 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 9\n\nSecond number: 1 | 7 | 1 | 8 | 8 | 4\n\nSo adding each column:\n\nOnes: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 8 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nThousands: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 499,923. But according to the step-by-step addition, it's 553,923. So there's a discrepancy. Therefore, my digit-wise addition is incorrect. But why?\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me check the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nWait, 382,139 is 3 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 2 thousands, 1 hundred, 3 tens, 9 ones.\n\n171,884 is 1 hundred thousand, 7 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 8 hundreds, 8 tens, 4 ones.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTen thousands: 8 + 7 = 15. So 15 ten thousands is 1,500,000.\n\nThousands: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nHundreds: 1 + 8 = 9\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11\n\nOnes: 9 + 4 = 13\n\nSo adding those:\n\n4 (hundred thousands) + 1,500,000 (ten thousands) + 3 (thousands) + 9 (hundreds) + 11 (tens) + 13 (ones)\n\nWait, but 1,500,000 + 3,000 + 900 + 110 + 13 = 1,503,913 + 13 = 1,503,926. Wait, that can't be. Wait, no, this approach is wrong because adding 15 ten thousands is 1,500,000, which is incorrect. Wait, no. Wait, 15 ten thousands is 15*10,000 = 150,000. So 4 (hundred thousands) is 400,000. 150,000 (ten thousands) is 150,000. 3 (thousands) is 3,000. 9 (hundreds) is 900. 11 (tens) is 1,100. 13 (ones) is 13. So total is 400,000 + 150,000 = 550,000; 550,000 + 3,000 = 553,000; 553,000 + 900 = 553,900; 553,900 + 1,100 = 555,000; 555,000 + 13 = 555,013. But that's not matching either. Wait, this is getting too confusing. Let me try a different approach.\n\nLet me use the standard addition algorithm step by step:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n  382,139\n\n+ 171,884\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 8 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10. Wait, hold on! Here's the mistake. Earlier, I thought the hundreds digits were 1 and 1, but actually, the first number's hundreds digit is 1, and the second number's hundreds digit is 8. So 1 + 8 = 9, not 10. But in my previous calculation, I added 1 + 1 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3. That's where the error was. I incorrectly took the hundreds digits as 1 and 1 instead of 1 and 8.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 8 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nThousands place: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the correct sum is 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 499,923. But wait, according to the step-by-step addition, it's 553,923. So there's still a discrepancy. What's going on here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, if we have:\n\nHundreds place: 1 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 9. So 9 in hundreds place.\n\nThousands place: 2 (from first number) + 7 (from second number) = 9. So 9 in thousands place.\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 9. So 9 in ten-thousands place.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 4. So 4 in hundred-thousands place.\n\nTens place: 3 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 11. So 1 in tens place and carry 1 to the thousands place. Wait, but we already added the thousands place. Wait, no, in the previous step-by-step addition, when adding the tens place, we had 3 + 8 = 11, wrote 2 and carried 1. But if the hundreds place addition was correct (1 + 8 = 9, no carry), then the thousands place addition should be 2 + 7 = 9, no carry. Then ten-thousands place 8 + 1 = 9, no carry. Hundred-thousands place 3 + 1 = 4. So the digits would be:\n\nHundred thousands: 4\n\nTen thousands: 9\n\nThousands: 9\n\nHundreds: 9\n\nTens: 1 (from 3 + 8 = 11, write 1 and carry 1, but wait, no. Wait, when adding the tens place, 3 + 8 = 11. So we write 1 and carry 1 to the thousands place. But the thousands place was already added as 2 + 7 = 9. Wait, but if we carry over 1 to the thousands place, then the thousands place becomes 9 + 1 = 10. Then we would have to carry over 1 to the ten-thousands place. But in the ten-thousands place, we added 8 + 1 = 9. So if we carry over 1 here, it would make ten-thousands place 9 + 1 = 10, which would carry over 1 to the hundred-thousands place. But the hundred-thousands place was 3 + 1 = 4. So adding 1 here would make it 5. But this contradicts the previous result.\n\nWait, this is getting too confusing. Let me try again, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nLet's add them step by step, aligning the digits:\n\nHundreds of thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\nFirst number: 3 | 8 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 9\n\nSecond number: 1 | 7 | 1 | 8 | 8 | 4\n\nAdding:\n\nOnes: 9 + 4 = 13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 8 = 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nThousands: 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3 + 1 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo the sum is 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). So 499,923.\n\nBut according to the step-by-step addition earlier, it's 553,923. So there's a conflict. Therefore, my digit-wise addition is wrong. But why?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the positions. Let me check the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 382,139\n\nSecond number: 171,884\n\nWait, 382,139 is 3 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 2 thousands, 1 hundred, 3 tens, 9 ones.\n\n171,884 is 1 hundred thousand, 7 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 8 hundreds, 8 tens, 4 ones.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nTen thousands: 8 + 7 = 15. 15 ten thousands is 150,000.\n\nThousands: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nHundreds: 1 + 8 = 9\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11\n\nOnes: 9 + 4 = 13\n\nAdding these together:\n\n4 (hundred thousands) + 150,000 (ten thousands) + 3,000 (thousands) + 900 (hundreds) + 1,100 (tens) + 13 (ones)\n\nCalculating:\n\n4 + 150,000 = 150,004\n\n150,004 + 3,000 = 153,004\n\n153,004 + 900 = 153,904\n\n153,904 + 1,100 = 155,004\n\n155,004 + 13 = 155,017\n\nWait, that's 155,017. That can't be right. But 382,139 + 171,884 should be around 553,923. So this method is also wrong. Therefore, my breakdown into thousands, hundreds, tens, ones is incorrect. Because when adding 15 ten thousands, that's 150,000, but adding 3,000, 900, etc., gives a total of 155,017, which is way too low. T