      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of two numbers: 743,486 and 802,977. Hmm, adding them together. Alright, let me start by writing them down one above the other to align the digits properly. \n\nSo, 743,486\nplus 802,977\n--------\nI'll add them column by column, starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place.\n\nFirst column (ones place): 6 + 7 = 13. I write down 3 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nSecond column (tens place): 8 + 7 = 15, plus the carried 1 makes 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\nThird column (hundreds place): 4 + 9 = 13, plus the carried 1 is 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nFourth column (thousands place): 3 + 2 = 5, plus the carried 1 is 6. Write down 6, no carry over.\n\nFifth column (ten-thousands place): 4 + 0 = 4. Write down 4.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check the digits again. The first number is 743,486. Breaking it down: 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 6 (ones). The second number is 802,977: 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (ones). \n\nSo when aligning, the digits are:\n\n7 4 3 4 8 6\n+8 0 2 9 7 7\n------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\nTens: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\nHundreds: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\nThousands: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\nTen-thousands: 4 + 0 = 4\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15\n\nWait, so the hundred-thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15. So that would be 5 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 carried over to the millions place. But since there are no more digits, we just add that 1. So the total sum is 1,556,463?\n\nWait, let me verify. Let me add them another way to check. Maybe breaking the numbers into parts.\n\n743,486 is 700,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 400 + 80 + 6.\n\n802,977 is 800,000 + 0 + 2,000 + 900 + 70 + 7.\n\nAdding the hundred thousands: 700,000 + 800,000 = 1,500,000.\n\nThen the ten thousands: 40,000 + 0 = 40,000.\n\nThousands: 3,000 + 2,000 = 5,000.\n\nHundreds: 400 + 900 = 1,300.\n\nTens: 80 + 70 = 150.\n\nOnes: 6 + 7 = 13.\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n1,500,000 + 40,000 = 1,540,000\n\n1,540,000 + 5,000 = 1,545,000\n\n1,545,000 + 1,300 = 1,546,300\n\n1,546,300 + 150 = 1,546,450\n\n1,546,450 + 13 = 1,546,463.\n\nHmm, so that gives me 1,546,463. Wait, but earlier when I added column by column, I got 1,556,463. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check the column addition again. Maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n743,486\n\n+802,977\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n  743,486\n\n+ 802,977\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\n8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\n4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\n3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\n4 + 0 = 4\n\n7 + 8 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nWait, hold on. The first number is 743,486. So the digits are:\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds)\n\n8 (tens)\n\n6 (ones)\n\nThe second number is 802,977:\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n9 (hundreds)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n7 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the ones place:\n\n6 + 7 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTens place: 8 + 7 = 15 + 1 = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1\n\nHundreds place: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 = 14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nThousands place: 3 + 2 = 5 + 1 = 6 \u2192 6, carry 0\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 + 0 = 4\n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n\nMillions place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo putting it all together: 1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, no. Wait, the places are:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 5\n\nTen thousands: 4\n\nThousands: 6\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 4\n\nOnes: 3\n\nWait, that would be 1,546,463. Wait, but earlier when I added the components, I got 1,546,463. So why did I think there was a discrepancy?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in my first calculation. Let me check again.\n\nFirst method: column addition:\n\nOnes: 6 +7=13 \u21923, carry 1\n\nTens:8 +7=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1\n\nHundreds:4 +9=13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1\n\nThousands:3 +2=5 +1=6 \u21926, carry0\n\nTen-thousands:4 +0=4\n\nHundred-thousands:7 +8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nMillions:1\n\nSo the total is 1,556,463. Wait, but when adding the components, I got 1,546,463. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the components again.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nBreakdown:\n\n700,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 400 + 80 +6\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nBreakdown:\n\n800,000 + 0 + 2,000 + 900 +70 +7\n\nAdding:\n\n700,000 +800,000=1,500,000\n\n40,000 +0=40,000\n\n3,000 +2,000=5,000\n\n400 +900=1,300\n\n80 +70=150\n\n6 +7=13\n\nAdding all:\n\n1,500,000 +40,000=1,540,000\n\n1,540,000 +5,000=1,545,000\n\n1,545,000 +1,300=1,546,300\n\n1,546,300 +150=1,546,450\n\n1,546,450 +13=1,546,463\n\nSo according to this, the total is 1,546,463.\n\nBut according to the column addition, I thought I got 1,556,463. Wait, maybe I messed up the column addition.\n\nWait, let's do the column addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nSecond number: 802,977\n\nLet me write them one above the other:\n\n743,486\n\n+802,977\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n6 +7 =13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place:8 +7=15 +1=16. Write 6, carry1.\n\nHundreds place:4 +9=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nThousands place:3 +2=5 +1=6. Write6, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands place:4 +0=4 +0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands place:7 +8=15. Write5, carry1.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. Wait, but according to the component addition, it's 1,546,463. There's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in one of the methods. Let's check with another approach. Let's use the standard algorithm step by step.\n\nFirst, write the numbers:\n\n   743,486\n\n + 802,977\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 +7 =13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place:8 +7=15 +1=16. Write6, carry1.\n\nHundreds place:4 +9=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nThousands place:3 +2=5 +1=6. Write6, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands place:4 +0=4. Write4.\n\nHundred-thousands place:7 +8=15. Write5, carry1.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,556,463. But according to the component method, it's 1,546,463. There's a difference of 10,000. Wait, where is that coming from?\n\nWait, let's check the ten-thousands place. The first number has 4 in the ten-thousands place, the second has 0. So 4 +0=4. That's correct.\n\nWait, but in the component method, when I added 40,000 +0=40,000. But 40,000 is part of the first number. Wait, but 743,486 is 700,000 +40,000 +3,000 +400 +80 +6. So 40,000 is correct. The second number is 802,977: 800,000 +0 +2,000 +900 +70 +7. So 0 in the ten-thousands place. So 40,000 +0=40,000. Then adding 5,000 (3,000 +2,000) gives 5,000. Then 1,300 (400 +900). Then 150 (80 +70). Then 13 (6 +7). So total is 1,500,000 +40,000 +5,000 +1,300 +150 +13. Let's compute that:\n\n1,500,000 +40,000 =1,540,000\n\n1,540,000 +5,000=1,545,000\n\n1,545,000 +1,300=1,546,300\n\n1,546,300 +150=1,546,450\n\n1,546,450 +13=1,546,463.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 1,556,463. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the column addition. Let me check again.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13. 3, carry 1.\n\nTens:8+7=15 +1=16. 6, carry1.\n\nHundreds:4+9=13 +1=14. 4, carry1.\n\nThousands:3+2=5 +1=6. 6, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands:4+0=4 +0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands:7+8=15. 5, carry1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. But according to the component method, it's 1,546,463. There's a difference of 10,000. Wait, where is that?\n\nWait, maybe the error is in the component method. Let's check the components again.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nBreakdown:\n\n700,000 +40,000 +3,000 +400 +80 +6\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nBreakdown:\n\n800,000 +0 +2,000 +900 +70 +7\n\nAdding:\n\n700,000 +800,000=1,500,000\n\n40,000 +0=40,000\n\n3,000 +2,000=5,000\n\n400 +900=1,300\n\n80 +70=150\n\n6 +7=13\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,500,000 +40,000=1,540,000\n\n1,540,000 +5,000=1,545,000\n\n1,545,000 +1,300=1,546,300\n\n1,546,300 +150=1,546,450\n\n1,546,450 +13=1,546,463.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 1,556,463. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with another method. Let's use the fact that 743,486 + 802,977 can be thought of as (700,000 + 800,000) + (40,000 +0) + (3,000 +2,000) + (400 +900) + (80 +70) + (6 +7). Which is 1,500,000 +40,000 +5,000 +1,300 +150 +13=1,546,463. So that's correct.\n\nBut according to column addition, why is it 1,556,463? There must be a mistake in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's do the column addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n      7  4  3  4  8  6\n\n    + 8  0  2  9  7  7\n\n--------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n6 +7 =13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place:8 +7=15 +1=16. Write6, carry1.\n\nHundreds place:4 +9=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nThousands place:3 +2=5 +1=6. Write6, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands place:4 +0=4 +0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands place:7 +8=15. Write5, carry1.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. Wait, but according to the component method, it's 1,546,463. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the component method. Let's check the ten-thousands place again. The first number has 4 in the ten-thousands place, the second has 0. So 4 +0=4. Then adding 5,000 (from thousands place). Wait, 4 (ten-thousands) +5,000. Wait, 4 in the ten-thousands place is 40,000. Then adding 5,000 would make it 45,000. But in the component method, I added 40,000 +5,000=45,000. Wait, but in the component breakdown, I had 40,000 (from first number) +0 (from second number)=40,000. Then added 5,000 (from thousands place). Wait, but the thousands place is 3 +2=5, which is 5,000. So 40,000 +5,000=45,000. Then adding 1,300 (400 +900). So 45,000 +1,300=46,300. Then 150 (80 +70). 46,300 +150=46,450. Then 13 (6 +7). 46,450 +13=46,463. Then adding to 1,500,000: 1,500,000 +46,463=1,546,463. So that's correct.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 1,556,463. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe the column addition is wrong. Let's check again.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13. 3, carry1.\n\nTens:8+7=15+1=16. 6, carry1.\n\nHundreds:4+9=13+1=14. 4, carry1.\n\nThousands:3+2=5+1=6. 6, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands:4+0=4+0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands:7+8=15. 5, carry1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. But according to component method, it's 1,546,463. So there's a difference of 10,000. Wait, how?\n\nWait, maybe the error is in the hundred-thousands place. The first number has 7 in the hundred-thousands place, the second has 8. So 7 +8=15. So we write 5 and carry 1. So the millions place becomes 1. So total is 1,556,463. But according to component method, it's 1,546,463. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 743,486 +802,977.\n\nFirst, 743,486 +800,000=1,543,486\n\nThen add 2,977: 1,543,486 +2,977=1,546,463.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the component method is correct. Therefore, the column addition must have an error.\n\nWait, so why does the column addition give 1,556,463? Let's check the steps again.\n\nFirst number: 743,486\n\nSecond number:802,977\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nTens:8+7=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nHundreds:4+9=13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nThousands:3+2=5 +1=6 \u21926, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands:4+0=4 +0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands:7+8=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. But according to the actual addition, it's 1,546,463. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe the hundred-thousands place. The first number has 7 in the hundred-thousands place, the second has 8. So 7 +8=15. So we write 5 in the hundred-thousands place and carry 1 to the millions place. So millions place becomes 1. So total is 1,556,463. But according to the component method, it's 1,546,463. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with the actual numbers. Let's compute 743,486 +802,977.\n\nLet me compute 743,486 +802,977:\n\nFirst, add 700,000 +800,000=1,500,000.\n\nThen add 40,000 +0=40,000.\n\nThen add 3,000 +2,000=5,000.\n\nThen add 400 +900=1,300.\n\nThen add 80 +70=150.\n\nThen add 6 +7=13.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,500,000 +40,000=1,540,000\n\n1,540,000 +5,000=1,545,000\n\n1,545,000 +1,300=1,546,300\n\n1,546,300 +150=1,546,450\n\n1,546,450 +13=1,546,463.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the component method is correct. Therefore, the column addition must have an error. Let's check the column addition again.\n\nWait, maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me write the numbers vertically properly:\n\n      743,486\n\n    + 802,977\n\n--------------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6 +7=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nTens:8 +7=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nHundreds:4 +9=13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nThousands:3 +2=5 +1=6 \u21926, carry0.\n\nTen-thousands:4 +0=4 +0=4.\n\nHundred-thousands:7 +8=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,556,463. But according to the component method, it's 1,546,463. So there's a conflict. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the component method. Let's check the ten-thousands place again. The first number has 4 in the ten-thousands place, the second has 0. So 4 +0=4. Then adding 5,000 (from thousands place). Wait, 4 (ten-thousands) is 40,000. Adding 5,000 gives 45,000. Then adding 1,300 (400 +900) gives 46,300. Then 150 (80 +70) gives 46,450. Then 13 (6 +7) gives 46,463. Then adding to 1,500,000 gives 1,546,463. That's correct.\n\nBut according to column addition, it's 1,556,463. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe the error is in the thousands place. Let's check:\n\nThousands place:3 (from first number) +2 (from second number)=5. Then carryover 0. So 5,000. But in column addition, we have 6 in the thousands place. Wait, no. Wait, the thousands place in the sum is 6. Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, the thousands place in the first number is 3, and in the second number is 2. So 3 +2=5. Then add the carryover 0, so 5. So the thousands digit in the sum is 5. But in the column addition, I wrote 6. That's the mistake!\n\nWait, in the thousands place, the first number is 3, the second is 2. 3 +2=5. Then add the carryover 0, so 5. So the thousands digit should be 5, not 6. That's where the error is.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nThousands place:3 +2=5 +0=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nSo the thousands digit is 5, not 6. Then the hundreds place:\n\nHundreds place:4 +9=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nWait, but if thousands place is 5, then the next digit is hundreds place. Wait, no. Wait, the columns are:\n\nOnes, Tens, Hundreds, Thousands, Ten-thousands, Hundred-thousands, Millions.\n\nSo after thousands place, the next is ten-thousands. So correcting the thousands place:\n\nFirst number:3 in thousands place.\n\nSecond number:2 in thousands place.\n\n3 +2=5. No carryover. So thousands digit is 5.\n\nBut in the initial column addition, I wrote 6. That's wrong. So the thousands digit should be 5, not 6. Therefore, the sum is:\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo 1,545,5463? Wait, no. Wait, let's reconstruct:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:3\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:4\n\nThousands:5\n\nTen-thousands:4\n\nHundred-thousands:5\n\nMillions:1\n\nSo the number is 1,545,5463? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo written out: 1,545,5463? Wait, no. Wait, the places are:\n\n1 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds